var tafsirData_5 = {
  "1": "“O believers! Honour your obligations.” A new chapter opens with a command that gathers contracts, covenants, and the known duties of faith. “Obligations” are the bonds a person has taken: with Allah, with a spouse, with an orphan, with a treaty-people, with a sale by mutual consent. The last chapter ended by making inheritance clear so people would not go astray. This one begins by saying: keep what you have bound yourselves with. A believer who prays and cheats a contract has not honoured the opening word.\n\n“All grazing livestock has been made lawful to you—except what is hereby announced to you and hunting while on pilgrimage.” Lawful food is the first example of a clear obligation. Grazing livestock is permitted. Exceptions will be listed. Hunting while in the pilgrim state is not allowed. The sacred journey has its own rules, as the last chapters had rules for shortened prayer and armed prayer. Holiness is not vagueness. It is honoured limits.\n\n“Indeed, Allah commands what He wills.” The permissions and bans are His command, not a committee’s taste. He wills lightness and He wills sacred exceptions. The next verse will protect the rituals of pilgrimage, the sacred months, the sacrificial animals, and the pilgrims themselves, then forbid letting old hatred make you transgress. Honouring obligations includes honouring people on their way to His House.\n\nHonouring obligations will be the thread of the coming stories: Israel’s covenant, Christian claims, and the believers’ own pledge of hearing and obeying. A person who keeps a sale and breaks a covenant with God has not understood the first sentence.",
  "2": "“O believers! Do not violate Allah’s rituals ˹of pilgrimage˺, the sacred months, the sacrificial animals, the ˹offerings decorated with˺ garlands, nor those ˹pilgrims˺ on their way to the Sacred House seeking their Lord’s bounty and pleasure.” The obligations of the first verse are now given a sacred map. Rituals of pilgrimage, sacred months, marked offerings, and pilgrims on the road are not to be violated. A person travelling to the House for bounty and pleasure is under a protection this verse creates. His journey is not a hunting season for an old enemy.\n\n“When pilgrimage has ended, you are allowed to hunt.” The ban on hunting was tied to the state and the time, not to meat itself. When the pilgrimage ends, the permission returns. Limits have edges. That is part of honouring them.\n\n“Do not let the hatred of a people who once barred you from the Sacred Mosque provoke you to transgress.” The believers had been barred from the Mosque. The wound was real. This verse forbids using that wound as a license. Hatred can feel like justice. It often becomes transgression. The next chapter-line will make the rule general.\n\n“Cooperate with one another in goodness and righteousness, and do not cooperate in sin and transgression. And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah is severe in punishment.” Cooperation is not always a virtue. It is a virtue in good. It is a crime in sin. Mindfulness decides which. Severity in punishment warns the person who would use a group to do what he would fear to do alone. The next verse will list forbidden foods, then announce that the faith has been perfected and Islam chosen as the way.",
  "3": "“Forbidden to you are carrion, blood, and swine; what is slaughtered in the name of any other than Allah; what is killed by strangling, beating, a fall, or by being gored to death; what is partly eaten by a predator unless you slaughter it; and what is sacrificed on altars.” The exceptions announced in the first verse are now spoken. Dead meat, blood, swine, and animals devoted to other than Allah are forbidden. Deaths without a lawful slaughter—strangling, beating, a fall, goring—are forbidden. A predator’s leftover is forbidden unless you reach it with a proper slaughter while life remains. Altar-sacrifices return to the pagan rites the last chapter condemned. The list is concrete because people hide unlawful meat behind vague words.\n\n“You are also forbidden to draw lots for decisions. This is all evil.” Lots here are the pagan arrows and games used to decide fate and divide meat. Decision by such chance is called evil along with the meats. A believer’s decisions are not to be handed to superstition.\n\n“Today the disbelievers have given up all hope of ˹undermining˺ your faith. So do not fear them; fear Me! Today I have perfected your faith for you, completed My favour upon you, and chosen Islam as your way.” This is among the last rulings revealed, in the Prophet’s farewell pilgrimage. Perfection of faith and completion of favour mean the religion has been given its standing form. Islam—submission—is the chosen way. Fear of the old enemies is replaced by fear of Allah.\n\n“But whoever is compelled by extreme hunger—not intending to sin—then surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” Lightness remains inside the perfect law. A starving person who eats to live, without wanting the sin, is under forgiveness. The next verse will answer what is permissible: what is good and lawful, and what trained hunting animals catch when Allah’s name is mentioned.",
  "4": "“They ask you, ˹O Prophet,˺ what is permissible for them ˹to eat˺. Say, ‘What is good and lawful.’” After a long forbidden list, people ask for the permitted. The answer is a principle before examples. Good and lawful: wholesome, not filthy, and allowed by Allah. The faith just perfected is not a religion of hunger. It is a religion that names evil and then names good.\n\n“Also what is caught by your hunting animals and birds of prey which you have trained as instructed by Allah. So eat what they catch for you, but mention the Name of Allah over it ˹first˺.” A trained hound or bird, taught in the way Allah instructed, may catch for you. The catch is for you, not a carcass you happen to find. Mentioning Allah’s name first keeps the hunt from becoming a pagan act or a thoughtless grab. The last chapter forbade eating what was dedicated to other than Allah. This verse puts His name back on the lawful hunt.\n\n“And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah is swift in reckoning.” Mindfulness at a meal and a hunt is easy to forget. Swift reckoning means the name skipped, the untrained animal used as a cover, or the forbidden meat renamed as good will not wait forever for a court.\n\nThe next verse will widen the table and the home. Today all good, pure foods are lawful. The food of the People of the Book is permissible, and yours for them. Chaste believing women and chaste women of the Scripture are permissible in marriage with dowries, in wedlock, not in fornication or as mistresses. Whoever rejects the faith voids his deeds.",
  "5": "“Today all good, pure foods have been made lawful for you.” “Today” continues the voice of the perfected faith. Good and pure foods are lawful. The religion is not a contest in forbidding what Allah made clean.\n\n“Similarly, the food of the People of the Book is permissible for you and yours is permissible for them.” A table can be shared with those given Scripture before, in the known lawful foods. This is a social mercy after many hard verses about their breaches. It is not a cancellation of those verses. It is a permission that keeps ordinary life from becoming a permanent boycott of neighbours who still hold a Book.\n\n“And ˹permissible for you in marriage˺ are chaste believing women as well as chaste women of those given the Scripture before you—as long as you pay them their dowries in wedlock, neither fornicating nor taking them as mistresses.” Chastity, dowry, and wedlock are the same fences built in the last chapter. Scripture-women are not lawful as secret pleasure. They are lawful as wives with rights. The last chapter’s care for women does not die at the border of the community.\n\n“And whoever rejects the faith, all their good deeds will be void ˹in this life˺ and in the Hereafter they will be among the losers.” The permissions sit under faith. A person who rejects after this clarity does not keep a private store of good that will save him. The next verse will teach how to rise for prayer: wash face and hands to the elbows, wipe the head, wash the feet, bathe after full impurity, and use clean earth when water cannot be found. The perfected way includes a purified standing before Allah.",
  "6": "“O believers! When you rise up for prayer, wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, wipe your heads, and wash your feet to the ankles.” The prayer that hypocrites did half-heartedly is here given its bodily door. Face, hands to the elbows, head wiped, feet to the ankles. The washing is an obligation of rising to prayer, not a decoration. A community with a perfected faith is taught how to stand clean.\n\n“And if you are in a state of ˹full˺ impurity, then take a full bath.” Full impurity requires more than the smaller wash. The last chapter already said not to approach prayer in that state until you have bathed, except passing through. This verse states the bath as part of the opening law of the chapter.\n\n“But if you are ill, on a journey, or have relieved yourselves, or have been intimate with your wives and cannot find water, then purify yourselves with clean earth by wiping your faces and hands.” The lightness of clean earth returns. Illness, travel, the call of nature, intimacy, and no water: the prayer is not dropped. Face and hands are wiped. “It is not Allah’s Will to burden you, but to purify you and complete His favour upon you, so perhaps you will be grateful.” Burden is denied. Purification, completed favour, and hoped-for gratitude are named as the will. The last verse’s completed favour is now felt in a dry riverbed where a person can still pray.\n\nThe next verse will say: remember Allah’s favour and the covenant when you said, “We hear and obey.” Be mindful. He knows what is hidden in the heart. The wash can be seen. The hearing and obeying must reach the hidden.",
  "7": "“Remember Allah’s favour upon you and the covenant He made with you when you said, ‘We hear and obey.’” Favour and covenant open the moral life after the wash. The favour is guidance, safety, a perfected way, a table of lawful good. The covenant is the pledge of hearing and obeying—the sentence hypocrites said and then undid at night. Believers are told to remember that they said it, and to remain inside it.\n\n“And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart.” Mindfulness again. The hidden heart is where “we hear and obey” either lives or dies. He knows best: better than the person who performs the wash and keeps a refusal inside, better than the community that sees only faces.\n\nThis verse ties the new chapter to the last. Obedience of the Messenger, referral of disputes, refusal of false judges, holding fast: all of that is the covenant of hearing and obeying. The next verse will apply it to testimony. Stand firm for Allah and bear true testimony. Do not let hatred of a people lead you to injustice. Be just. That is closer to righteousness. The covenant is tested when you hate the other side.\n\nRemembering the covenant is especially needed after a verse about earth-wiping and water. Outer purity can become a costume. The hidden heart is where ‘we hear and obey’ is either a living sentence or a quote from the past.\n\nA person can wash and still break a sale, a treaty, or a marriage right. This verse sends him from the water back to the pledge. Hearing and obeying was not said to the limbs only. It was said to the heart Allah knows.",
  "8": "“O believers! Stand firm for Allah and bear true testimony.” The last chapter said stand firm for justice as witnesses for Allah, even against yourselves and parents. This verse says stand firm for Allah and bear true testimony. The two commands are one spine. Testimony is for Him. Standing is for Him. A perfected faith still needs this old steel.\n\n“Do not let the hatred of a people lead you to injustice. Be just! That is closer to righteousness.” Hatred was already forbidden as a cause of transgression at the Sacred Mosque. Now it is forbidden as a cause of crooked witness. You may have been barred, mocked, or fought. You may not lie, cheat a scale, or deny a right because you hate the person. “Be just” is a short shout in the middle of the verse. Closer to righteousness means justice is not a cold extra. It is nearer to the fear of Allah than a loyalty that lies for the group.\n\n“And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah is All-Aware of what you do.” Awareness covers the hidden lean in a testimony, the extra word that tips a case, the silence that helps your side. The next two verses will promise believers who do good forgiveness and a great reward, and will name those who disbelieve and deny the signs as residents of the Fire. Justice in hatred is one of the goods attached to that promise.\n\nThe believers who were once barred from the Mosque are the first audience, but the rule outlives them. Every later hatred—tribe, nation, sect—will try to hire the tongue. This verse refuses the hire. Be just. That is closer to righteousness.",
  "9": "“Allah has promised those who believe and do good ˹His˺ forgiveness and a great reward.” After commanding justice even toward a hated people, the Quran names the pay. Forgiveness and a great reward: the two gifts this Book keeps pairing. Justice against your desire is hard. Forgiveness covers what remains. The great reward honours the hardness.\n\nBelief and good deeds are again the people of the promise. Good here includes honouring contracts, leaving the forbidden, washing for prayer, keeping “we hear and obey,” and refusing to let hatred write a testimony. The promise is from Allah, so it is not a hope hung on a mood.\n\nThe next verse will set the other residence. Those who disbelieve and deny Our signs are the residents of the Hellfire. Promise and residence, garden-language and fire-language, continue to travel as a pair. A community that has just been told its faith is perfected is not told that the two ends have merged. They have not. The just witness and the denier of signs do not sleep in the same home.\n\nA great reward after a command to be just toward enemies is well placed. People often think righteousness is heat against the hated. Allah places the reward on those who believe, do good, and keep the scale straight when the heart is hot.\n\nForgiveness is named first because even a just witness still needs covering. The great reward is named second so that justice will not feel like a loss. Allah pays the person who refused to let hatred hire his tongue.",
  "10": "“As for those who disbelieve and deny Our signs, they are the residents of the Hellfire.” The promise of the last verse is given its shadow. Disbelief joined to denial of signs—the verses, the proofs, the clear limits just listed—makes people residents of the Fire. Resident is the old word for a lasting stay. They are not described as visitors.\n\nDenial of signs in this opening can mean denying the lawful and unlawful, denying the perfected way, or denying the Messenger who announced them. The first verses of the last chapter said whoever denies Allah, His angels, His Books, His messengers, and the Last Day has gone far astray. This verse names the home of that denial.\n\nThe next verse will remind the believers of a favour in this world, not only of homes in the next. A people sought to harm you, but He held their hands back. Be mindful, and put your trust in Him. The residents of the Fire are not the only story. There is also the story of hands that were raised against the believers and then held back by Allah.\n\nResidents of the Hellfire is a hard line after the kindness of lawful food and shared tables. The kindness was never a blurring of ends. A neighbour may eat with you. A denier of the signs still has a residence named, if he dies on the denial.\n\nThe next verse will remind the believers of a favour in this world: hands raised to harm them were held back. Fire is one of Allah’s acts. Protection is another. Both belong to the same Lord they were told to fear instead of their old enemies.",
  "11": "“O believers! Remember Allah’s favour upon you: when a people sought to harm you, but He held their hands back from you.” The opening of the chapter now becomes a memory of rescue. Reports mention plots against the Prophet and his companions—an enemy reaching for a weapon, a guest planning a strike—and Allah stopping the hand. The wording is wider than one story. People sought harm. He held their hands. The believers who were told not to fear those who lost hope of undermining the faith are given a concrete favour to remember.\n\n“Be mindful of Allah. And in Allah let the believers put their trust.” Mindfulness and trust close the passage as they closed so many passages before. Mindfulness is the inner answer to a completed favour. Trust is the answer to a held-back hand. If He can stop a hand, He can be trusted with the next one. The last chapter said if Allah helps you, none can defeat you. This verse is that help remembered as a favour, not as a theory.\n\nThe next verses will turn to older covenants, with the Children of Israel and with the Christians, and will show how covenants are kept or broken. Honouring obligations, the chapter’s first word, will be read across nations and centuries. The believers who just remembered a held-back hand are about to be shown people who were given a covenant and then broke it, so that they will not treat their own “we hear and obey” as a sentence without a history.",
  "12": "“Allah made a covenant with the Children of Israel” is where the chapter now turns to the older nations. The previous verse ended with the believers being told to put their trust in Allah. Now the Quran shows what happens to a people who received a covenant from Allah and then broke it. A covenant is a binding agreement. In an earlier chapter, Allah says to the Children of Israel, “Fulfil your covenant and I will fulfil Mine,” and the same spirit appears here. The covenant is not a one-sided promise. It carries conditions, and the verse lists them one by one.\n\nThe verse then says Allah “appointed twelve leaders from among them.” The Children of Israel were divided into twelve tribes, and each tribe was given a leader, sometimes called a chief or a guardian. Another passage of the Quran points to the same twelve tribes when it says that twelve springs gushed out from a rock, and “each tribe knew its drinking place.” These leaders were responsible for their people, just as every person who is placed over others carries a duty.\n\nThen Allah says, “I am truly with you.” This is the heart of the covenant: Allah’s support is with those who keep their side. But the support is tied to action. The verse names that action: “If you establish prayer, pay alms-tax, believe in My messengers, support them, and lend to Allah a good loan.” Prayer and alms-tax are the daily duties of worship and charity. Believing in the messengers and supporting them means accepting those whom Allah sent and helping them in their mission. The phrase “lend to Allah a good loan” is striking: spending wealth in Allah’s way is described as a loan given to Allah Himself. The same idea appears in another chapter, which asks, “Who will lend to Allah a good loan which Allah will multiply many times over?” Giving is not a loss; it is a loan that Allah repays.\n\nIn return, Allah promises: “I will certainly forgive your sins and admit you into Gardens under which rivers flow.” Forgiveness and gardens are the two gifts that the Quran keeps pairing for those who keep their promises. The verse then closes with a warning: “And whoever among you disbelieves afterwards has truly strayed from the Right Way.” Disbelief after receiving the covenant is not a small slip; it is leaving the straight road entirely. The next verse will show what happened when that warning was ignored.",
  "13": "“But for breaking their covenant, We condemned them and hardened their hearts.” The word “but” turns the discussion from the covenant itself to what the people did with it. The previous verse described the agreement and its promise. This verse shows the other side: the Children of Israel broke the covenant, and the Quran says plainly what followed. “We condemned them” means Allah drove them away from His mercy by their own choice. The condemnation is a result, not a random punishment, because it came after they broke their word.\n\nThen comes “hardened their hearts.” A hardened heart no longer accepts guidance easily. In an earlier chapter the Quran tells the same people, “Even then your hearts became hardened like a rock or even harder.” The hardening is described as a consequence of their own repeated refusal. When a person keeps turning away from the truth, the heart slowly becomes hard, until truth no longer moves it.\n\nThe verse then lists what they did: “They distorted the words of the Scripture and neglected a portion of what they had been commanded to uphold.” Distorting the words means changing the meaning of what Allah revealed. Another passage says of them, “Some Jews take words out of context” and yet another says a group of them “would hear the word of Allah then knowingly corrupt it after understanding it.” Neglecting a portion of the command means abandoning parts of their religion while claiming to keep it.\n\nThen the Prophet is told: “You ˹O Prophet˺ will always find deceit on their part, except for a few.” This is a warning about dealing with them: some will try to deceive, though a few remained faithful. The verse ends with an instruction: “But pardon them and bear with them. Indeed, Allah loves the good-doers.” At this stage of the mission, the Muslims were commanded to forgive and to be patient with the harm they received. Scholars explain that this command belonged to the period before the ruling about fighting was given, and that later verses changed the situation. But the closing words remain a standing lesson: “Indeed, Allah loves the good-doers.” Patience and pardon, when they are the right response, are themselves a form of goodness that Allah loves.",
  "14": "“And from those who say, “We are Christians,” We took their covenant” continues the same discussion, moving from the Children of Israel to another community. Notice the wording: the verse does not even use a neutral name for them. It describes them by their claim: those who say, “We are Christians,” They call themselves Christians, and they were given a covenant just as the Children of Israel were. This shows that the covenant with Allah is not limited to one nation. Every community that received a messenger and a scripture was bound by a duty.\n\nThe verse then says, “but they neglected a portion of what they had been commanded to uphold.” The same words were used in the previous verse about the Children of Israel. But there is a difference worth noticing: for the Children of Israel, the Quran mentions distortion of the Scripture and hardening of the heart. For the Christians, the main failure named here is neglect — leaving aside part of what they were commanded. The Quran does not treat every community in exactly the same way, because their situations were not exactly the same.\n\nThen comes the result: “So We let hostility and enmity arise between them until the Day of Judgment.” Commentators explain that this refers to the divisions among the Christian groups themselves, which split into many sects that disagreed and even fought one another. The history of those communities is full of exactly this: groups carrying the same name, divided by bitter hostility toward each other. Some scholars also see in these words the enmity that arose between them and others, but the well-known meaning is the division among themselves. A community that neglects its covenant loses its unity.\n\nThe verse closes with “and soon Allah will inform them of all they have done.” Nothing they did is lost. On the Day of Judgment, Allah will make clear to them everything they did in this world, and they will face the truth of their own actions.",
  "15": "“O People of the Book! Now Our Messenger has come to you” is a direct address to both Jews and Christians together. The word “now” is important. It points to this moment of revelation and to the arrival of Muhammad as the messenger who completes the line of earlier messengers. The title “People of the Book” honours them with the name of the scriptures they received, while at the same time calling them to face the new message.\n\nThe verse says the Messenger has come “revealing much of what you have hidden of the Scriptures and disregarding much.” This tells us two things. First, the Quran exposes much of what the People of the Book had concealed. Scholars mention that among the hidden matters were descriptions of the final Prophet and rulings that they had buried. Second, the Quran disregards much — it does not dig up every hidden matter. There is wisdom in this: the goal is not to shame people but to guide them. Allah reveals what people need for their guidance and leaves aside what would only cause harm.\n\nThen comes one of the great descriptions of the Quran: “There certainly has come to you from Allah a light and a clear Book.” The Quran is called a light because it illuminates what was dark: the truth about Allah, about guidance, about what is lawful and unlawful. Another chapter tells the believers, “So believe in Allah and His Messenger and in the Light We have revealed.” The Book is also called “clear” because it does not speak in riddles; it explains what people need to know and calls them to a way they can understand.\n\nThe next verse will continue the sentence and explain what this light and this clear Book actually do. It guides those who seek Allah’s pleasure, brings them out of darkness, and leads them to the Straight Path. So the light is not only a description; it is a working light, one that moves people from ignorance to knowledge and from misguidance to guidance.",
  "16": "“through which Allah guides those who seek His pleasure” continues the sentence from the previous verse. The words “through which” point back to the light and the clear Book. The Quran is not described as something to be admired from a distance. It is a tool of guidance, and the verse names who receives that guidance: those who seek Allah’s pleasure. The condition is on the seeker. The same light reaches many ears, but it enters hearts that genuinely want the truth. A person who does not want to be guided will find the Book hard to follow; a person who sincerely seeks Allah’s pleasure will find in it a path.\n\nThen comes “to the ways of peace.” Peace here means safety and salvation, both in this life and in the life to come. Guidance leads to ways of peace: peace with Allah, peace within oneself, and peace with others when people truly follow the Book.\n\nThe verse continues: “brings them out of darkness and into light by His Will.” The same image appears in an earlier chapter, where the Quran says, “Allah is the Guardian of the believers—He brings them out of darkness and into light.” Darkness is a picture of ignorance, error, and fear. Light is a picture of knowledge, truth, and safety. Notice the phrase “by His Will”: the guidance is Allah’s doing. People seek, but Allah guides. Both parts matter — the sincere seeking is the human side, and the guiding is the divine side.\n\nThe verse ends with “and guides them to the Straight Path.” This is the very path the believers ask for in the opening chapter of the Quran, when they pray, “Guide us along the Straight Path.” Here the Quran answers that prayer: this is what the Book does for those who seek His pleasure. The Straight Path is the middle road — the road of the prophets and the truthful, the road that avoids both neglect and excess.",
  "17": "“Indeed, those who say, “Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,” have fallen into disbelief.” The verse does not begin with a debate. It begins with a clear verdict. The claim that the Messiah, Jesus son of Mary, is Allah Himself is described as disbelief. This is not a matter where the Quran leaves room for doubt. The same teaching appears in another chapter, which tells the People of the Book, “Do not go to extremes regarding your faith; say nothing about Allah except the truth. The Messiah, Jesus, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger of Allah.” Another chapter records the Christian claim beside the Jewish claim that a human being is the son of Allah, and yet another describes the claim that Allah has offspring as an outrageous claim “by which the heavens are about to burst.” The Quran repeats this correction in many places because the error is serious.\n\nThen the Prophet is told to say: “Who has the power to prevent Allah if He chose to destroy the Messiah, son of Mary, his mother, and everyone in the world all together?” This question is the heart of the argument. If Jesus were Allah, no one could touch him. But the question forces every listener to admit that Allah has full power over Jesus, over his mother Mary, and over every creature. Anything that can be destroyed by Allah is a creature, not the Creator. The question needs no answer, because the answer is obvious.\n\nThe verse then states the basis of the argument: “To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything in between. He creates whatever He wills.” Jesus was created by Allah’s will — born from a mother without a father, which is a miracle of Allah’s power, not a sign of divinity. “He creates whatever He wills” includes creating Jesus by the word “Be.”\n\nThe verse ends with “And Allah is Most Capable of everything.” Nothing is beyond His power — neither creating Jesus, nor destroying him, nor raising him. The One who is Most Capable of everything needs no son and no partner, because nothing is too hard for Him alone.",
  "18": "“The Jews and the Christians each say, “We are the children of Allah and His most beloved!”” The previous verse dealt with one false claim about Jesus. This verse deals with another claim, and this time it is shared by both communities. Both groups claimed a special closeness to Allah — that they are His children and His most beloved. The verse does not leave the claim without an answer. The Prophet is told to say: “Why then does He punish you for your sins?” This is a simple, powerful question. If they were truly Allah’s children in the sense they meant, and His most beloved, why did punishment fall on them in this world, and why do their own scriptures warn them of punishment in the next? Punishment for sin and a belovedness of that kind cannot stand together.\n\nThen comes the correction: “No! You are only humans like others of His Own making.” They are human beings, created by Allah, exactly like the rest of humanity. Being close to Allah is not a matter of birth or of claiming a title. It is a matter of obedience and faith.\n\nThe verse then says, “He forgives whoever He wills and punishes whoever He wills.” This is Allah’s right alone. No community can guarantee itself forgiveness by claiming a special name. Another chapter says, “Indeed, Allah does not forgive associating others with Him ˹in worship˺, but forgives anything else of whoever He wills.” Forgiveness and punishment belong to Allah, not to any people’s claim.\n\nThe verse closes with the same words that closed the previous one: “To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything in between. And to Him is the final return.” The repetition is meaningful. Both verses refute false claims, and both end by returning everything to Allah. The kingdom is His, and the final return is to Him. In the end, every claim of special rank will be tested by what a person actually did — not by what he called himself.",
  "19": "“O People of the Book! Our Messenger has indeed come to you, making things clear to you after an interval between the messengers” — the word “interval” means a gap, a long stretch of time in which no messenger was sent. Between Jesus and Muhammad there was a long period, reported in the books of history as roughly six hundred years, during which no prophet came to the world. The Prophet himself pointed to this closeness between himself and Jesus. In a hadith recorded in both Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim, he said that he is the closest of people to Jesus, son of Mary, in this world and the Hereafter, and that there was no prophet between them.\n\nWhy does the verse mention this interval? The next words explain: “so you do not say, “There has never come to us a deliverer of good news or a warner.”” The gap in time could have become an excuse. A people could say: we waited, but no one came; no one warned us; we had no chance. The coming of Muhammad removes that excuse completely. No one can say that no deliverer of good news or warner reached them.\n\nThen the verse confirms: “Now there has come to you a deliverer of good news and a warner.” The two titles describe the work of the messengers. A deliverer of good news announces the reward of obedience — paradise and Allah’s pleasure. A warner announces the danger of disobedience — the Fire and Allah’s displeasure. Muhammad carried both messages together, like the prophets before him. He did not come only to frighten or only to please. He came with the full message.\n\nThe verse ends with “And Allah is Most Capable of everything.” This phrase has now appeared at the end of several verses in this chapter. It reminds the listener that sending messengers, sealing the excuse, and judging the people are all within Allah’s power. The One who sent the Messenger at the right time is the One who will hold everyone to account.",
  "20": "“And ˹remember˺ when Moses said to his people” — the chapter now moves from directly addressing the People of the Book to retelling the history of the Children of Israel under Moses. The word “remember” calls the listeners to recall this story, because it holds lessons for them. The address begins with “O my people!” — Moses speaks to his own nation with tenderness, not as a stranger.\n\nHe tells them: “O my people! Remember Allah’s favours upon you when He raised prophets from among you, made you sovereign, and gave you what He had never given anyone in the world.” Three favours are named. First, Allah “raised prophets from among you” — the Children of Israel received many prophets from their own ranks. Second, He “made you sovereign” — He gave them rule and kings, after they had been slaves in Egypt. Third, He “gave you what He had never given anyone in the world” — the special gifts of that time, such as the manna and quails that came down from heaven, and the cloud that shaded them in the desert, as another chapter describes: “We shaded you with clouds and sent down to you manna and quails.” The same call appears in earlier chapters, where Allah Himself says, “O Children of Israel! Remember ˹all˺ the favours I granted you and how I honoured you above the others.”\n\nThe pattern is worth noticing. Allah’s own words to the Children of Israel begin with the same command to remember, and here Moses repeats that command to his people. The messengers simply repeat what Allah commanded: remind people of His favours, so that gratitude may lead them to obedience.\n\nThis opening prepares the ground for what follows. The next verse will record the command that came with those favours: enter the Holy Land. A people who remember Allah’s favours should find it easy to obey His command. The story will show what happened when they did not.",
  "21": "“O my people! Enter the Holy Land which Allah has destined for you ˹to enter˺.” This is the command that followed the reminder of favours in the previous verse. Moses first reminded his people of what Allah had given them, and now he calls them to act: enter the Holy Land. The Holy Land is the sacred land, the region of Jerusalem and Palestine, which the commentators describe as the land made holy by the prophets and by Allah’s blessing. The phrase “which Allah has destined for you” means that Allah had written and decreed this land for them. It was not a random conquest. It was the fulfilment of a promise — the land promised to the descendants of Abraham.\n\nBut notice how the command is tied to their side of the bargain. The land was destined for them, yet they still had to enter it. A promise from Allah does not remove human effort. The destined land still had to be walked into, settled, and protected. This is a deep lesson: divine decree and human action work together. The destination was fixed, but the road had to be travelled.\n\nThen Moses adds: “And do not turn back or else you will become losers.” Turning back here means retreating from the command out of fear. The word “losers” is strong: those who turn back lose the land, lose the promise, and lose the reward of obedience. In the next verses, the people will indeed turn back, and the story will show exactly what their loss looked like.\n\nThe same command appears in another form in an earlier chapter, where the Quran says to the Children of Israel, “Enter this city and eat freely from wherever you please; enter the gate with humility.” There the entry is tied to humility and repentance. Here it is tied to courage and trust. Both are needed: the humility of a servant entering his Lord’s gift, and the courage to walk into it without turning back.",
  "22": "“They replied, “O Moses! There is an enormously powerful people there, so we will never ˹be able to˺ enter it until they leave.”” This is the answer of the people to the clear command of the previous verse. Moses had told them to enter the Holy Land and warned them not to turn back. Their reply is an excuse built on fear. They admit that the land is inhabited by a powerful people. Commentators mention reports that the city they were meant to enter was guarded by mighty warriors, and some reports describe them as a strong nation of that region. The exact identity of that people is not the point of the story. The point is their excuse.\n\nThe wording “an enormously powerful people” shows that their fear had an object. They were not imagining danger; there really was a strong enemy. But that is exactly the test. The land was promised by Allah, and the people measured the promise against the enemy instead of measuring the enemy against Allah.\n\nThen they add: “If they do, then we will enter!” They made their obedience conditional: we will enter only when the enemy leaves. This turns the command upside down. They were commanded to enter and trust Allah, but they demanded that the difficulty be removed first. Faith works the other way: trust Allah first, then act. Their words show a heart that wants the promise without the effort, and the reward without the risk.\n\nThe next verse will show that not everyone among them spoke this way. Two men who feared Allah will stand up and call the people to the gate. But the majority had already made up their minds.",
  "23": "“Two God-fearing men—who had been blessed by Allah—said, “Surprise them through the gate.”” After the people’s fearful reply in the previous verse, two men stand up and speak differently. The verse describes them with two honours: they were “God-fearing” and they had been “blessed by Allah.” Fear of Allah made them fearless of the enemy. Commentators report that these two were among the leaders of the people, and many reports of the scholars name them as Yusha‘ ibn Nun, who later became the prophet sent to the Children of Israel after Moses, and Kalab ibn Yufanna. The Quran itself does not name them here, but their description is enough: they feared Allah and were blessed by Him.\n\nTheir plan is short and confident: “Surprise them through the gate. If you do, you will certainly prevail.” They do not promise an easy war. They point to the gate — the point of entry — and urge their people to move with courage and determination. Their confidence is not built on their own strength. It is built on the promise of Allah.\n\nThen they say the words that carry the whole lesson of the story: “Put your trust in Allah if you are ˹truly˺ believers.” This connects directly to an earlier verse of this chapter, which ended with the words, “And in Allah let the believers put their trust.” Trust in Allah is not a slogan. It is what belief looks like when a command is difficult. The two men are telling their people: if your faith is real, show it by trusting Allah and walking through the gate.\n\nThe offer is put before the people. But the next verse will record their answer, and it shows that the majority chose their fear over their faith. The two God-fearing men were right, but the people would not follow them.",
  "24": "“˹Yet˺ they said, “O Moses! ˹Still˺ we will never enter as long as they remain there.”” The word “yet” sets this verse against the previous one. Two God-fearing men had just called the people to the gate and told them to trust Allah. The people do not answer with a debate or a plea. They answer with refusal, and the verse states it twice: “we will never enter.” Their refusal is absolute, and it is tied to the presence of the enemy: “as long as they remain there.” The condition of their obedience is that the obstacle must disappear by itself. They will not move until the enemy moves.\n\nThen comes their most shocking sentence: “So go—both you and your Lord—and fight; we are staying right here!” These words show how deep their rebellion had become. They speak to Moses as if they are dismissing both the prophet and his Lord. The words “both you and your Lord” separate Allah and His messenger from themselves in the same breath. A believer would never speak this way to a prophet. But this is exactly what the Quran wants its listeners to see: what happens to a people who are given clear commands and clear promises, yet refuse out of fear and stubbornness.\n\nWith “we are staying right here” they chose the desert over the promised land, and the slavery of the heart over the struggle of faith. Their decision is recorded not to amuse us, but to warn every generation: a person can know the truth, receive the promise, and still lose it by refusing to act. The next verses will show how Moses and Allah answered this refusal.",
  "25": "“Moses pleaded, “My Lord! I have no control over anyone except myself and my brother.”” After the people’s open defiance in the previous verse, Moses does not argue with them or try to force them. He turns to Allah. The word “pleaded” shows the tone: the prophet humbling himself before his Lord, admitting his weakness. He says, “I have no control over anyone except myself and my brother.” This is a confession of the prophet’s limits. Moses could deliver the message, but he could not force hearts. His brother here is Aaron, the prophet whom Allah had appointed to help him. Out of a whole nation, Moses says, only himself and his brother stood with him in obedience.\n\nThen he says: “So set us apart from the rebellious people.” The words “set us apart” carry the meaning of separation and judgement. Moses is asking Allah to separate him and his brother from the rebellious ones — to spare them from sharing in the fate of a people who refused. Some commentators explain it as a request to judge between them; others as a request to be saved from being counted among them. Either way, the prophet distances himself from their rebellion and leaves their affair to Allah.\n\nThis verse is a lesson in how a leader of truth behaves when people refuse: he does not become a tyrant, and he does not surrender his own obedience. He states the truth, does his duty, and hands the matter to Allah. The next verse will bring the divine answer: the land becomes forbidden to the rebellious people for forty years.",
  "26": "“Allah replied, “Then this land is forbidden to them for forty years, during which they will wander through the land.”” The divine answer comes directly after Moses’ plea in the previous verse. The land that was promised to the Children of Israel is now forbidden to this generation. The word “forbidden” is strong: not delayed, not difficult — forbidden. The people who refused to enter out of fear will not enter at all. Instead of the Holy Land, they will wander through the desert for forty years. The punishment fits the crime. They turned back from the command, so they will spend their lives going around in circles, never reaching the destination. The generation that said “we will never enter” died without entering.\n\nScholars explain that the sentence fell on that defiant generation of adults. Their children, who had not refused, were the ones who later entered the land under the leadership of Yusha‘, the very man who had urged the people to trust Allah in this story. In this way, the promise of Allah was not cancelled; it was inherited by a generation that was willing to obey. Another chapter points to this when it says, “We made the oppressed people successors of the eastern and western lands, which We had showered with blessings.”\n\nThen Allah says to Moses: “So do not grieve for the rebellious people.” The prophet is told not to waste his grief on those who chose their own destruction. There is a lesson here for every caller to the truth: you deliver the message, you do your duty, but you do not carry the guilt of those who refuse. Their loss is their own choice.\n\nThe story of Moses and the wandering generation is finished. The next verses will move to another story — the story of Adam’s two sons — to teach another lesson about sin, envy, and the value of a human life.",
  "27": "“Relate to them in truth ˹O Prophet˺ the story of Adam’s two sons” — the Quran now moves from the story of Moses to an even older story, the first murder in human history. The command to relate “in truth” tells the Prophet to tell the account as it really was, not as legend. The names of Adam’s two sons are not given in the Quran itself, but the commentators report them as Qabil and Habil, known in English as Cain and Abel. They were sons of Adam, brothers of the first human family.\n\nThe verse continues: “how each offered a sacrifice: Abel’s offering was accepted while Cain’s was not.” Each brother brought an offering to Allah. The Quran does not say exactly what each one offered, and the commentators differ in their reports about it — some say one brought the best of his animals while the other brought inferior produce. What matters is the result: one offering was accepted and the other was not. Acceptance of an offering belongs to Allah alone, and it is not measured by the size of the gift.\n\nThen comes the reaction: “So Cain threatened, “I will kill you!”” Envy burned in Cain’s heart. He could not bear that his brother’s offering was accepted and his own was not. The threat of murder came from jealousy — the same disease that has torn families apart ever since. The verse does not soften it or explain it away. It shows the ugliness of envy plainly.\n\nAnd Abel’s answer is recorded in the same verse: “His brother replied, “Allah only accepts ˹the offering˺ of the sincerely devout.”” The words translated as “sincerely devout” carry the meaning of taqwa — awareness of Allah, piety, godliness. Abel tells his brother the truth: the problem is not in Allah’s judgement; it is in the heart that brought the offering. When a person’s heart is not right with Allah, no gift can be right.\n\nThe next verses will show what happened after this exchange. And the Prophet’s own teaching records that this first murder left a burden on the first son of Adam, because he was the first to begin the killing of human beings.",
  "28": "“If you raise your hand to kill me, I will not raise mine to kill you, because I fear Allah—the Lord of all worlds.” Abel’s answer continues from the previous verse. His brother has threatened to kill him, and Abel replies with words that have become a standing example of patience. He refuses to become a killer, even in self-defence. His fear of Allah is greater than his fear of death, and he will not let his brother’s crime drag him into a crime of his own.\n\nThe phrase “I fear Allah—the Lord of all worlds” is the reason he gives. The title “Lord of all worlds” appears again in the opening chapter of the Quran, where Allah is praised with the same words. Abel sees his brother and himself as two creatures standing before the Lord of everything. How could he lift his hand in murder while standing in front of that Lord?\n\nIt is important to understand this story correctly. Abel’s choice is his own act of piety in that particular situation. It does not mean that Islam forbids a person from ever defending his life. The Quran permits defending against aggression, and the coming verses of this same chapter will set the punishment for those who take life unjustly. What the story of Abel shows is the highest degree of godliness: a man who would rather be killed than become a killer, because his fear of Allah ruled his heart completely.\n\nThe verse leaves the two brothers face to face: one with a raised hand, the other with a lowered one. The next verse will record Abel’s final words before the crime takes place.",
  "29": "“I want to let you bear your sin against me along with your other sins, then you will be one of those destined to the Fire.” Abel’s final words continue from the previous verse. He has refused to fight back, and now he explains what will happen if his brother goes through with the murder. He is not saying that he wishes evil for his brother. He is saying that by refusing to fight back, he will not share in the sin. The killer will carry the sin of killing him, added to whatever other sins he has.\n\nThis is a deep teaching about sin and blame. When a person is wronged and does not take revenge, the wrongdoer carries the full weight of his own crime. He cannot say that the victim shared the blame. Abel is telling his brother: if you kill me, the sin is entirely yours. I will not give you the excuse of a fight.\n\nThen comes the result: “then you will be one of those destined to the Fire.” Abel speaks of the punishment with certainty, because killing without right is a crime that leads to the Fire. And he adds the general rule: “And that is the reward of the wrongdoers.” This is not only about his brother. It is a universal statement. Wrongdoing has a reward, and that reward is the Fire, unless the wrongdoer repents and Allah forgives him.\n\nThe verse shows how a victim of injustice can speak: with truth, without cursing, without despair. Abel names the consequence, warns his brother, and leaves the matter with Allah. The next verse will record the murder itself.",
  "30": "“Yet Cain convinced himself to kill his brother, so he killed him—becoming a loser.” The story reaches its dark centre. The word “yet” turns from Abel’s gentle warning to the crime that followed. “Cain convinced himself” — the phrase describes an inner battle. His own self urged him on and made the crime seem easy. The Quran uses this kind of language about the human self elsewhere, showing how the self can push a person toward evil until the wrong action feels natural. Cain did not kill in a sudden madness. He talked himself into it. The murder was planned in the heart before it was done with the hand.\n\nThe verse then states the act with terrible brevity: “so he killed him” — the first murder in human history, a brother killed by his own brother, the first blood spilled on the earth. The Prophet spoke of this in a hadith recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim: whenever a person is killed unjustly, the first son of Adam carries a share of the sin, because he was the first to begin the killing of human beings. Every unjust killing since that day carries a trace of that first crime, which is why the coming verses of this chapter will declare how seriously Allah views the taking of a single life.\n\nThe verse ends with “—becoming a loser.” Cain lost in both worlds at once. He lost his brother, he lost his peace, he lost his standing before Allah, and he gained nothing but the memory of what he had done. A moment of envy bought him a permanent loss. The next verse will show his regret, and the strange way Allah taught him to bury his brother’s body.",
  "31": "“Then Allah sent a crow digging ˹a grave˺ in the ground ˹for a dead crow˺, in order to show him how to bury the corpse of his brother.” After the murder, a strange and powerful scene follows. Cain is left with the body of his brother and does not know what to do with it. It is the first death of a human being, and burial is unknown. So Allah sends a crow that digs into the ground. Many commentators mention reports describing two crows that fought, and when one was killed, the other dug the earth and buried it, so that Cain could see with his own eyes what to do. The Quran itself does not give those details; it simply says Allah sent a crow digging in the ground to show Cain how to bury his brother’s corpse.\n\nThis is a lesson in itself. Allah taught the first murderer through a bird what he could not figure out for himself. The crow became a teacher, and the man who had killed his brother had to watch a bird to learn how to honour the dead body. Human beings were created with honour, and even the body of a murdered man had a right to be covered in the earth.\n\nThen Cain cries out: “Alas! Have I ˹even˺ failed to be like this crow and bury the corpse of my brother?” The word “alas” is a cry of regret. He compares himself to the crow and finds himself lower. The brother he envied, the brother he killed, now cannot even be buried without the example of a bird.\n\nThe verse ends: “So he became regretful.” But notice what the regret is about. The Quran does not say he repented and was forgiven. It says he became regretful — sorry for what he had done, or sorry for the trouble it brought him. Regret is not the same as repentance. True repentance turns to Allah, leaves the sin, and seeks His mercy. Cain’s regret is left hanging, as a warning that feeling sorry is not enough when the heart does not turn back to Allah. The very next verse will draw the great lesson from this entire story: the value of a single human life.",
  "32": "“That is why We ordained for the Children of Israel” — the words “that is why” point straight back to the story of Adam’s two sons in the previous verses. After the first murder in human history, Allah laid down a great rule for the Children of Israel. The rule is about the value of a single life. Another passage reminds the same people of the covenant they once gave: “We took your covenant that you would neither shed each other’s blood nor expel each other from their homes.” Now this verse raises the value of life even higher.\n\nThe verse says: “whoever takes a life—unless as a punishment for murder or mischief in the land—it will be as if they killed all of humanity.” Notice the two exceptions. Taking a life is forbidden, except as a lawful punishment for murder, or as punishment for spreading mischief in the land. Those exceptions are the lawful penalties that protect society. But killing without right is described in the heaviest way possible: as if the killer destroyed all of humanity. The meaning is not that the numbers are equal. It is that the sanctity of every single life is so great that violating one life is like violating the whole human family.\n\nThen the verse says: “and whoever saves a life, it will be as if they saved all of humanity.” Saving a life means rescuing a person from death — whether by protecting him, feeding him, or pulling him away from danger. Some early scholars also understood it to mean: whoever refrains from killing a soul that Allah has forbidden, it is as if he gave life to all people. Either way, the honour of saving one person is immense. This is why the Prophet taught that the first matter to be settled among people on the Day of Resurrection will be cases of bloodshed — an authentic teaching recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim.\n\nThe verse ends with a sad note: “˹Although˺ Our messengers already came to them with clear proofs, many of them still transgressed afterwards through the land.” Despite the great rule and the clear proofs, many still went beyond the limits. The next verse will describe the penalty for those who wage war and spread mischief in the land.",
  "33": "“Indeed, the penalty for those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and spread mischief in the land” is a command about a particular kind of criminal: the armed outlaw who terrorizes the road and attacks people. The phrase “wage war against Allah and His Messenger” does not mean that humans can literally fight Allah. It means they fight Allah’s religion and His community — they violate His law, shed blood, and spread corruption. The verse then lists the penalties: “death, crucifixion, cutting off their hands and feet on opposite sides, or exile from the land.”\n\nScholars explain that the type of punishment matches the crime. One who only terrorizes and frightens people may be exiled. One who robs without killing may have his hands and feet cut from opposite sides. One who kills may be executed. One who kills and takes wealth may be crucified. The schools of law differ over some details of how these penalties are matched to the crimes, but the principle is agreed: the judge applies the fitting punishment for the harm the criminal caused. The verse calls these penalties “a disgrace for them in this world, and they will suffer a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter” — unless they repent, as the very next verse will show.\n\nThe occasion of these verses is connected with an incident reported in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim. A group of people from the tribes of Ukl and Uraynah came to Medina and accepted Islam, but the climate made them ill. The Prophet directed them to the camels of charity to drink their milk. Instead of showing gratitude, they killed the shepherd, drove away the camels, and left the religion. When they were captured, the Prophet punished them for their crime. Commentators mention this incident when explaining these verses, because it shows exactly the kind of armed treachery the Quran is describing.\n\nThe verse teaches that the community has the right — and the duty — to protect the lives and property of its people. Mercy toward society includes stopping those who terrorize it.",
  "34": "“As for those who repent before you seize them” — after the severe penalties of the previous verse, the Quran opens a door of escape. The criminals described there — those who wage war and spread mischief — are not locked into their punishment if they repent in time. The words “before you seize them” set the condition: the repentance must come before the authorities capture them. Scholars explain that if the outlaw surrenders and turns back before being overpowered, the worldly punishment is dropped. The person who comes in repentance, with his weapon put down and his crime confessed, is treated as one who has returned.\n\nThen comes the beautiful conclusion: “then know that Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The verse does not only remove the worldly punishment. It points beyond the world, to Allah’s forgiveness of the sin itself. Sincere repentance can erase the crime between the servant and his Lord. This is a mercy even for the worst kind of criminal — as long as the repentance is real and comes while there is still a choice.\n\nThe same spirit appears later in this chapter, in the verse about the thief: “But whoever repents after their wrongdoing and mends their ways, Allah will surely turn to them in forgiveness.” Both verses teach the same lesson: Allah’s mercy is not defeated by human evil. What matters is the turning back, the changing of the way, and the sincere intention not to return to the crime.\n\nScholars note that repentance after capture does not cancel the punishment in this world, because the harm to society has already happened and the penalty protects others. But even then, repentance between the person and Allah remains accepted if it is sincere. The believer should never despair of Allah’s mercy, and should never use Allah’s mercy as an excuse to keep sinning.",
  "35": "“O believers! Be mindful of Allah and seek what brings you closer to Him” — after speaking of penalties, repentance, and the fate of criminals, the Quran turns back to the believers with a call to action. “Be mindful of Allah” is the foundation: to live with awareness that Allah sees, hears, and knows. Then comes the phrase “seek what brings you closer to Him.” The word used here is the same word the Prophet used for a special rank in Paradise. In a hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim, the Prophet taught his followers to ask Allah, when they hear the call to prayer, to grant him al-wasilah — and he explained that al-wasilah is a rank in Paradise that only one servant of Allah will reach, and he hoped to be that one. So the word carries a beautiful double meaning: the means of nearness to Allah in this life, and the highest nearness in the life to come.\n\nHow does a servant seek nearness to Allah? Through the deeds He loves — prayer, charity, kindness, truthfulness, and obedience to His commands. Another verse of the Quran describes even the beings people call upon as “seeking a way to their Lord, hoping for His mercy, and fearing His punishment.” If the closest of Allah’s creatures seek a way to Him, then humans must do the same.\n\nThen the verse continues: “and struggle in His Way, so you may be successful.” The word struggle covers more than fighting. It includes striving against laziness, against the self’s desires, and against the forces of evil — with effort, wealth, and time. The goal is stated at the end: success. Not the success of wealth or status, but the success of drawing near to Allah and winning His pleasure.\n\nThe verse gathers the whole path in one sentence: awareness of Allah, seeking nearness to Him through good deeds, and struggling in His way. Whoever follows these three has been promised success.",
  "36": "“As for the disbelievers, even if they were to possess everything in the world twice over ˹and offer it all˺ to ransom themselves from the punishment of the Day of Judgment, it would never be accepted from them.” After the previous verse promised success to the believers, this verse shows the fate of the other side. Imagine owning the whole world — and then double it. That is what the verse offers as a picture: all the wealth of the earth twice over, offered as a ransom. And still it would not be accepted.\n\nWhy? Because on the Day of Judgment, wealth has no buying power. The ransom that is needed there is faith and good deeds brought from this life. Nothing can be bought after death. The same idea appears in another chapter: “Indeed, if each of those who disbelieve then die as disbelievers were to offer a ransom of enough gold to fill the whole world, it would never be accepted from them.” Notice the words “then die as disbelievers.” The door of exchange closes at death. While a person is alive, repentance is possible; after death, no amount of gold can help.\n\nThe verse ends: “And they will suffer a painful punishment.” The pain is not only the punishment itself, but the knowledge that they had everything and could have used it — yet they spent it on what did not benefit them, and now even the greatest ransom is refused.\n\nThere is a warning here for everyone, not only for disbelievers. It is easy to trust in wealth, influence, or excuses. But the Day of Judgment accepts only what was sent ahead: faith, good deeds, and repentance offered while there was still time.",
  "37": "“They will be desperate to get out of the Fire but they will never be able to.” The pronoun “they” reaches back to the disbelievers of the previous verse. Their punishment is not only painful — it is also hopeless. The verse pictures them wanting to leave the Fire, desperately. But the door does not open. Another passage of the Quran paints the same scene: “Whenever they try to escape from Hell—out of anguish—they will be forced back into it, ˹and will be told,˺ ‘Taste the torment of burning!’” Every attempt to leave is turned back. And another chapter says: “Whenever their skin is burnt completely, We will replace it so they will ˹constantly˺ taste the punishment.” The pain never ends, and the body is renewed so the tasting never stops.\n\nThe verse then says: “And they will suffer an everlasting punishment.” The word “everlasting” is important. The punishment is not a period with an ending. This is the fate of those who die upon disbelief, after the truth reached them and they rejected it. The Quran is clear that this lasting punishment belongs to unbelief, not to every sin. A believer who sins may be punished or forgiven, but a disbeliever who dies on disbelief faces this eternal loss.\n\nWhy does the Quran repeat such pictures of the Fire? Because warning and hope belong together. The previous verses opened the door of repentance for criminals. These verses close the door of escape for those who reject the truth itself. A person who reads the Quran is being addressed while he is still alive — while the door is still open. The Fire is described so that no one will walk into it with open eyes. The same chapter that shows the Fire’s despair also shows, just before it, the mercy that accepts repentance before capture.",
  "38": "“As for male and female thieves, cut off their hands for what they have done—a deterrent from Allah.” The verse now moves from the punishment of the Hereafter to a specific punishment in this world. The Quran speaks of both men and women — the law applies to everyone equally, rich or poor, noble or common. The punishment is cutting off the hand, and the verse gives the reason: it is “a deterrent from Allah.” The goal is not cruelty; the goal is protection. When the punishment is known and applied, the hand that would steal is stopped, and the wealth of people is made safe.\n\nThe Prophet applied this law without favouritism. In a hadith recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim, a woman from the tribe of Makhzum stole, and some people tried to intercede for her, thinking her noble status would spare her. The Prophet became angry and said that the nations before them were destroyed because when the noble stole they let him go, but when the weak stole they punished him — and he added, by Allah, if his own daughter Fatima stole, he would cut off her hand. This is the meaning of justice: the law does not bow to status.\n\nThe verse ends with “And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.” The punishment is not a human invention. It comes from the Almighty, so no one can object to His authority; and it comes from the All-Wise, so it is placed exactly where it fits. Islamic law surrounds this punishment with strict conditions: the thief must have stolen in secret from safekeeping, the stolen item must reach a certain value, and the proof must be solid. These conditions show that the law intends justice, not haste.\n\nA society that protects property but forgets mercy becomes hard; a society that shows mercy but forgets justice becomes unsafe. The Quran keeps both.",
  "39": "“But whoever repents after their wrongdoing and mends their ways, Allah will surely turn to them in forgiveness.” The word “but” is a door of mercy opening right after the punishment of the previous verse. The thief is not beyond hope. The verse does not say the punishment will never apply — it speaks about the person’s standing with Allah. If the thief truly repents after the crime and changes his behaviour, Allah will accept his repentance.\n\nThe words “mends their ways” are important. Repentance is not just saying “I am sorry.” It is a change in direction: the person stops stealing, returns to honesty, and becomes a different kind of person. The Quran often pairs repentance with reform. A repentance that ends in the same sin is a statement, not a repentance.\n\nThen comes the promise: “Indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The same two names appeared at the end of the verse about the bandits who repent before capture. The Quran keeps pairing forgiveness and mercy because they are the two qualities the sinner needs most: forgiveness to erase the past, and mercy to receive him back.\n\nScholars discuss how repentance relates to the worldly punishment. If the matter has already reached the ruler and the punishment has been established, most scholars hold that repentance does not cancel the punishment in this world — the community’s right must still be fulfilled — though Allah’s acceptance of the repentant heart remains. Some scholars also discuss whether repentance before the case reaches the ruler can drop the punishment, following the pattern of the verse about the bandits. The differing views show that this is a matter where scholars have genuinely differed, and the details are left to the scholars of law. What every Muslim can hold onto is certain: sincere repentance, with changed behaviour, is never rejected by Allah.",
  "40": "“Do you not know that the kingdom of the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah ˹alone˺?” The question is not asked to get information. It is asked to wake the listener up. Of course the kingdom belongs to Allah. But the verse wants this truth to sit at the centre of how we think about Allah’s laws. The punishment of the thief, the acceptance of repentance, the judgement of the Day — all of it comes from the One who owns everything. No one can object to His judgment, because no one shares His kingdom.\n\nThen the verse continues: “He punishes whoever He wills and forgives whoever He wills.” This is Allah’s right alone. He punishes with perfect justice and forgives with perfect mercy — and both are wise. No ruler, no tribe, and no nation can claim the right to decide whom Allah must forgive or punish. The same words appeared earlier in this chapter, in the reply to those who claimed to be Allah’s beloved children: “He forgives whoever He wills and punishes whoever He wills.”\n\nThe verse ends with “And Allah is Most Capable of everything.” This phrase closes many verses in this chapter, like a drumbeat after every major statement. He is Most Capable of cutting off the hand of a thief, Most Capable of forgiving a repentant heart, Most Capable of punishing on the Day of Judgment, and Most Capable of guarding His kingdom. Nothing is too hard for Him.\n\nThere is a practical lesson here. When a person feels that a ruling of Allah is too harsh or too lenient, the answer of this verse is: the kingdom is His. Our limited view cannot measure His wisdom. The believer’s position is trust — knowing that the Owner of everything has arranged everything with knowledge and wisdom.",
  "41": "“O Messenger! Do not grieve for those who race to disbelieve” — the verse begins by comforting the Prophet. Some people were rushing toward disbelief as if competing in a race, and their behaviour could have weighed heavily on his heart. So Allah tells him not to grieve for them. The same comfort appears in another chapter: “˹O Prophet!˺ Do not grieve for those who race to disbelieve—surely they will not harm Allah in the least.” Their race harms only themselves.\n\nThe verse then describes them: “those who say, ‘We believe’ with their tongues, but their hearts are in disbelief.” These are the hypocrites — faith on the tongue, unbelief in the heart. Their danger is that they appear to be believers while working against the believers.\n\nThen the verse turns to another group: “Nor those among the Jews who eagerly listen to lies, attentive to those who are too arrogant to come to you.” Commentators explain that some Jews listened eagerly to falsehood and paid attention to their own leaders who were too proud to come to the Prophet themselves. The verse then exposes their method: “They distort the Scripture, taking rulings out of context, then say, ‘If this is the ruling you get from Muhammad, accept it. If not, beware!’” They changed the meaning of their own Book and then judged the Prophet’s ruling by their desires: if it matched what they wanted, they accepted it; if not, they rejected it. This was exactly their behaviour in the famous case of adultery, when they had changed the Torah’s ruling of stoning — as will be explained in the coming verses.\n\nThe verse continues: “Whoever Allah allows to be deluded, you can never be of any help to them against Allah.” The Prophet cannot force guidance into a heart that Allah has left to its own choice because of its stubbornness. And the verse says clearly: “It is not Allah’s Will to purify their hearts.” Purity of heart comes when a person wants it and turns to Allah. For those who persist in distortion, the result is stated plainly: “For them there is disgrace in this world, and they will suffer a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter.”",
  "42": "“They eagerly listen to falsehood and consume forbidden gain.” The pronoun “they” points to the same people described in the previous verse — those among the Jews who raced against the truth. The verse names two habits: listening eagerly to lies, and devouring what is forbidden. Commentators mention reports that some of their scholars listened to their own people’s lies and took bribes and other forbidden wealth. The two habits belong together: when a person loves falsehood, he will also love unlawful gain, because both come from the same corrupt root.\n\nThen the verse gives the Prophet a choice: “So if they come to you O Prophet, either judge between them or turn away from them.” The people of the Book could bring their disputes to the Prophet voluntarily. Allah allowed him to judge between them if he wished, or to decline. Scholars explain that this choice was given to the ruler of the Muslims in such cases. The verse adds the reassurance: “If you turn away from them, they cannot harm you whatsoever.” The Prophet had no reason to fear them or to please them.\n\nBut if he chose to judge, the condition is clear: “But if you judge between them, then do so with justice. Surely Allah loves those who are just.” Justice is not allowed to bend because the parties are Jews or Christians. Another verse commands: “when you judge between people, judge with fairness.” And an earlier verse of this very chapter told the believers: “Do not let the hatred of a people lead you to injustice. Be just! That is closer to righteousness.”\n\nThe verse teaches a lasting principle for judges and rulers: the door of judgement may be opened or closed by policy, but once a judge sits to judge, justice is the only law.",
  "43": "“But why do they come to you for judgment when they already have the Torah containing Allah’s judgment, then they turn away after all?” The question is a rebuke. These people brought their cases to the Prophet, but they already had their own Book from Allah with a clear ruling. Their problem was not a missing law. Their problem was that they did not like the law. The famous story behind these verses is reported in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim. A Jewish man and woman committed adultery, and the Jews disagreed about what to do with them. Some of them came to the Prophet hoping he would give a lighter ruling. The Prophet asked them what their Torah said about adultery. They said it prescribed flogging and humiliation. But a Jewish scholar named Abdullah ibn Salam told them they were lying and demanded that the Torah be brought. When it was opened, the verse of stoning was found. So the Prophet judged by the Torah itself and ordered that the two be stoned. And the narrator of the hadith, Abdullah ibn Umar, said he saw the man shielding the woman from the stones.\n\nThis story shows the meaning of the verse. The Torah “contained Allah’s judgment” — the stoning — but they had changed it to flogging. They came to the Prophet not to find the truth, but to find a ruling that suited them. When he ruled by the very Book they claimed to follow, they turned away. Hence the verse’s closing words: “They are not true believers.” Their coming was not an act of faith; it was an attempt to shop for a lighter law.\n\nThe story also shows the honesty of the Prophet’s judging: he judged by Allah’s revelation, even when it was severe, even when the people hoped for something softer. And it shows that the earlier verse’s warning — “If this is the ruling you get from Muhammad, accept it. If not, beware!” — described exactly this behaviour: accept only what matches desire, and reject the rest.",
  "44": "“Indeed, We revealed the Torah, containing guidance and light, by which the prophets, who submitted themselves to Allah, made judgments for Jews.” The verse honours the Torah: it was guidance for the people and light in their darkness. And it honours the prophets: they judged by it, and they were people who submitted themselves to Allah. Then the verse mentions the scholars: “So too did the rabbis and scholars judge according to Allah’s Book, with which they were entrusted and of which they were made keepers.” The rabbis and scholars were not owners of the Book; they were trustees. They were entrusted with it and made its keepers — a heavy responsibility. They were to preserve it, act by it, and judge by it.\n\nThen comes the great command: “So do not fear the people; fear Me!” This is addressed to all who carry knowledge of Allah’s Book. The scholar who fears people will bend the truth to please them; the scholar who fears Allah will speak the truth even when it costs him. And with it: “Nor trade my revelations for a fleeting gain.” The same warning appears in an earlier chapter: “Do not be the first to deny them or trade them for a fleeting gain.” A fleeting gain — a small worldly benefit — is not worth selling the words of Allah. This was the very sin of those who changed the ruling of stoning for money, status, or comfort.\n\nThen comes the verse’s most discussed sentence: “And those who do not judge by what Allah has revealed are truly the disbelievers.” A famous statement attributed to Ibn Abbas explains that this is “disbelief less than disbelief” — meaning it does not necessarily take a person out of Islam. The scholars explain: if a person judges by other than Allah’s law while believing that Allah’s law is the truth and that his own action is wrong, he commits a grave sin — a kind of disbelief in action — but he does not leave the religion. But if he believes that the other law is better, or equal, then that is true disbelief that leaves the religion. This is why the next two verses will repeat the same warning with different words: “wrongdoers” and “rebellious.” The judge who strives for the truth is not forgotten, for the Prophet said that the judge who strives and is correct receives two rewards, and the judge who strives and errs receives one — a teaching recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim.",
  "45": "“We ordained for them in the Torah, “A life for a life, an eye for an eye, a nose for a nose, an ear for an ear, a tooth for a tooth—and for wounds equal retaliation.”” This is the law of equal retaliation, ordained in the Torah. Its purpose is justice: the punishment matches the harm, no more and no less. The Quran later gave the Muslim community a similar law for murder — another chapter says: “˹The law of˺ retaliation is set for you in cases of murder— a free man for a free man, a slave for a slave, and a female for a female.”\n\nThen the verse opens the door of mercy: “But whoever waives it charitably, it will be atonement for them.” The victim or his guardians may forgive the retaliation. If they waive their right charitably — out of goodness, not weakness — that forgiveness becomes an atonement for their own sins. The Prophet taught that mercy is not a loss: the one who forgives is not robbed; he is purified. Scholars explain that the one who forgives the retaliation receives the reward of charity and the expiation of sins, while the offender still owes what the law requires — for murder, the blood money if the guardians accept it, and for wounds, compensation where it applies.\n\nThe verse ends with the same warning as the previous one, with a different word: “And those who do not judge by what Allah has revealed are truly the wrongdoers.” The word “wrongdoers” points to the injustice done to others when the law is set aside. The previous verse called such judging disbelief; this one calls it wrongdoing; the coming verse will call it rebellion. Scholars explain these three descriptions as degrees of the same fault: it is a serious sin that includes elements of unbelief, injustice, and defiance — and how far it goes depends on the heart of the person, as was explained in the previous verse.\n\nThe verse as a whole teaches balance: the law of equal justice protects society, and the door of forgiveness protects mercy. Neither cancels the other.",
  "46": "“Then in the footsteps of the prophets, We sent Jesus, son of Mary, confirming the Torah revealed before him.” The line of prophets continues. The words “in the footsteps” mean Jesus followed the prophets of Israel, walking in their path and carrying their message. His mission did not cancel the Torah; it confirmed it. Jesus came to affirm the truth of what was revealed before him and to call his people back to it. Another chapter describes the same sending: “Then in the footsteps of these prophets, We sent Our messengers, and after them We sent Jesus, son of Mary, and granted him the Gospel.”\n\nThen the verse describes the Gospel: “And We gave him the Gospel containing guidance and light and confirming what was revealed in the Torah—a guide and a lesson to the God-fearing.” Notice the words: the Gospel contained guidance and light — the same words used earlier in this chapter for the Torah, which was described as “containing guidance and light.” Both Books came from the same source, and both pointed people to the same God. The Gospel also confirmed the Torah, because it did not come to cancel the truth of what came before.\n\nThe phrase “a guide and a lesson to the God-fearing” shows the purpose of the Gospel: to guide those who fear Allah — those whose hearts are open — and to remind them of their duties. It was not a license to abandon the earlier law, but a renewal of the call to Allah.\n\nThis prepares the way for the coming verses. If Jesus confirmed the Torah and the Gospel carried guidance and light, then the people who received them were expected to follow them. The next verse will state that expectation clearly, and the verse after that will turn to the final Book — the Quran — which confirms and guards all that came before it.",
  "47": "“So let the people of the Gospel judge by what Allah has revealed in it.” The command given to the Jews is now given to the Christians. Just as the prophets and rabbis judged by the Torah, the people of the Gospel were commanded to judge by what Allah revealed in it. The verse does not speak of the Gospel as a memory or a decoration; it speaks of it as a law to be followed. Those who claimed to follow Jesus were expected to live by the Book they claimed to hold.\n\nThis verse is connected to the previous one, which described the Gospel as “containing guidance and light.” A Book with guidance and light must be taken seriously. If the people of the Gospel had truly followed its guidance, they would have followed what it confirmed — including the coming of the final Messenger, whom the previous scriptures described. Their failure to live by their own Book is the very neglect this chapter has been exposing.\n\nThen comes the third repetition of the warning: “And those who do not judge by what Allah has revealed are truly the rebellious.” The previous two verses said such people are “the disbelievers” and “the wrongdoers.” Here they are called “the rebellious” — those who defy Allah’s authority openly. The three verses use three words for the same fault: disbelief in the heart, wrongdoing in action, and rebellion against authority. Scholars explain that the three descriptions do not contradict one another; they show how serious the fault is from every angle. And as explained before, the degree depends on the person’s heart: judging by another law while believing Allah’s law is the truth is a grave sin; judging by another law while believing it is equal or better is true unbelief.\n\nThere is also a lesson in the order of the verses: the Torah was judged by, then the Gospel, then comes the Quran — the final authority, which the next verse will describe as standing as “a supreme authority” over the earlier Scriptures.",
  "48": "“We have revealed to you O Prophet this Book with the truth, as a confirmation of previous Scriptures and a supreme authority on them.” The verse now turns to the Quran itself. It was revealed “with the truth” — its news is true, its laws are true, its promises and warnings are true. And it has two relations to the earlier Books: it confirms them, agreeing with what remains true in them; and it stands as a “supreme authority” over them — the word carries the meaning of a guardian and judge. The Quran is the final referee: what agrees with it is accepted, and what disagrees with it is set aside.\n\nThen the command: “So judge between them by what Allah has revealed, and do not follow their desires over the truth that has come to you.” The Prophet is commanded to judge by revelation, not by the desires of the people — even when they push and argue. The next verse will repeat this command with even stronger warning.\n\nThen comes a profound statement: “To each of you We have ordained a code of law and a way of life.” Each nation of prophets had its own law and its own way — different rules for different times. But the religion — the core of faith, the worship of one God — was one. The Prophet said: the prophets are paternal brothers; their mothers are different, but their religion is one — a teaching recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim. The mothers are the different laws; the one religion is the worship of Allah alone.\n\nThe verse then explains why the laws differ: “If Allah had willed, He would have made you one community, but His Will is to test you with what He has given each of you.” Another chapter says the same: “Had Allah willed, He could have easily made you one community ˹of believers˺.” The differences in law were a test of obedience, not a license for conflict. Therefore: “So compete with one another in doing good.” The same command appears in another chapter: “So compete with one another in doing good. Wherever you are, Allah will bring you all together ˹for judgment˺.” The competition is not about which nation is superior — it is about who does more good. And the verse closes: “To Allah you will all return, then He will inform you of the truth regarding your differences.” The disputes will be settled by the One who knows the truth.",
  "49": "“And judge between them O Prophet by what Allah has revealed, and do not follow their desires.” The command of the previous verse is repeated, and the repetition matters. When the Quran repeats a command, it usually means the matter is serious and the danger of slipping is real. The Prophet had already been told to judge by revelation; now he is told again, with an added warning: “And beware, so they do not lure you away from some of what Allah has revealed to you.”\n\nThe word “beware” shows that the pressure was real. The people of the Book were skilled negotiators. In the case of the adulterous couple, they came hoping the Prophet would rule by flogging instead of stoning — that is, they tried to pull him away from part of what Allah had revealed. The Quran warns him to watch for such attempts, and to hold firmly to the full revelation, not to a convenient part of it.\n\nThen comes the reassurance: “If they turn away from Allah’s judgment, then know that it is Allah’s Will to repay them for some of their sins, and that many people are indeed rebellious.” If they reject the ruling, the Prophet should not be disturbed. Their turning away is not a failure of his mission — it is itself a punishment from Allah for some of their sins. Their rejection of the truth becomes part of their own account, and Allah repays them for what they did.\n\nThe verse teaches a lasting lesson for all who carry the truth: do not bend the message to please listeners. Those who dislike the truth may pressure, argue, or leave — but the messenger’s duty is only to convey what was revealed, fully and honestly. And there is comfort in the end of the verse: when people turn away, it is not a mystery and not a defeat; it is part of Allah’s just dealing with those who persist in rebellion.",
  "50": "“Is it the judgment of pre-Islamic ignorance they seek?” The question carries a tone of amazement and rebuke. After all that was said about judging by Allah’s revelation — in the Torah, in the Gospel, and in the Quran — some people still wanted something else. The Quran calls that something else “the judgment of pre-Islamic ignorance.” The phrase “pre-Islamic ignorance” refers to the time before Islam, when people followed false customs, tribal pride, and human desires instead of Allah’s guidance. The same phrase appeared earlier in the Quran, in the description of those who “were disturbed by evil thoughts about Allah—the thoughts of ˹pre-Islamic˺ ignorance.” To seek judgment outside what Allah revealed is to reach back to that dark time — as if all the light of revelation had not come.\n\nThe question is aimed first at the people of the Book and the hypocrites of that time, who preferred their own desires to Allah’s judgment. But the question reaches every generation. Whenever a person or a society prefers its own rules, its own desires, or its own customs over the clear judgment of Allah, the same question stands: is this the judgment of ignorance being sought?\n\nThen comes the answer in the form of a question: “Who could be a better judge than Allah for people of sure faith?” The answer is obvious: no one. For “people of sure faith” — those whose belief is firm and certain — the matter is settled. They do not shop for judgments. They know that the One who created them, knows them, and loves them is the best judge of what is good for them. Human judgments change with moods, interests, and times; Allah’s judgment is fixed in wisdom.\n\nThe verse does not say that Muslims may not use human laws or courts for worldly affairs. It says that the final authority, the reference point, and the judge of what is right and wrong is Allah and His revelation. Whoever keeps that reference point stands in the light; whoever abandons it steps back toward the ignorance that the Quran came to remove.",
  "51": "“O believers! Take neither Jews nor Christians as guardians—they are guardians of each other.” The verse now gives the believers a clear rule about loyalty. The word translated “guardians” carries the meaning of allies, protectors, and those whose side you take. The believers are told not to take the Jews and the Christians as guardians in this sense. The verse then explains why the warning is sharp: “they are guardians of each other” — their solidarity runs against the believers, so a believer who attaches himself to them as a guardian is leaning on a support that is not on his side.\n\nThen comes the severe warning: “Whoever does so will be counted as one of them.” The one who takes them as guardians in religious loyalty becomes counted among them. This is a warning, not a statement about a person’s final fate — but it is a warning of the highest seriousness. Another chapter states the same principle: “Believers should not take disbelievers as guardians instead of the believers—and whoever does so will have nothing to hope for from Allah—unless it is a precaution against their tyranny.” And another says: “O believers! Do not take disbelievers as allies instead of the believers. Would you like to give Allah solid proof against yourselves?”\n\nThe verse ends: “Surely Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.” The one who gives his loyalty to the enemies of the believers while wronging his own community places himself in the ranks of the wrongdoers, and Allah’s guidance does not reach those who choose wrongdoing.\n\nIt is important to understand this verse correctly, because it is often misunderstood. It does not forbid kindness, trade, marriage, neighbourliness, or peaceful life with Jews and Christians. Another verse of this very Book says: “Allah does not forbid you from dealing kindly and fairly with those who have neither fought nor driven you out of your homes.” What the verse forbids is loyalty that harms the Muslim community — taking them as protectors against the believers, preferring their side in a conflict of faith, or surrendering religious independence to them. Commentators mention that the verse was revealed at a time when some Muslims in Medina still had old alliances with Jewish tribes, and it cut those loyalties at the root. The following verse exposes those with “sickness in their hearts” who said, “We fear a turn of fortune will strike us” — showing that the warning was aimed at hearts whose loyalty was divided.",
  "52": "“You see those with sickness in their hearts racing for their guardianship” — the verse takes the warning of the previous verse and shows who was really listening to it. The previous verse told the believers not to take the Jews and Christians as guardians. Now we see a group “racing” to do exactly that — running toward that guardianship as if competing for it. The description “sickness in their hearts” is the Quran’s phrase for hypocrisy and weak faith. An earlier chapter says of such people: “There is sickness in their hearts, and Allah ˹only˺ lets their sickness increase.” The sickness is doubt, and it grows when it is fed.\n\nTheir excuse is quoted: “We fear a turn of fortune will strike us.” They were afraid that the Muslims would lose, that the tide would turn against them, and that their old Jewish and Christian allies would then protect them. So they kept a foot in both camps — professing Islam while holding on to the other side, in case Islam failed. This is the mind of the hypocrite: loyalty is sold to whoever seems strongest, not to the truth.\n\nThe verse answers them: “But perhaps Allah will bring about your victory or another favour by His command, and they will regret what they have hidden in their hearts.” The believers did not need their insurance policy. Allah would bring victory to the Muslims, or another favour — commentators mention the conquest of Khaybar among the events that exposed the hypocrites — and then those who had hidden loyalty to the other side would regret what they had concealed. The verse teaches that those who hedge their faith against the future will find their calculations turned upside down. Allah’s promise to His believers does not depend on the fears of the faint-hearted.",
  "53": "“Only then, the believers will ask one another, “Are these the ones who swore solemn oaths by Allah that they were with you?”” The words “only then” point to the moment of victory described in the previous verse. When Allah brings the victory or the favour, the believers will look back at the hypocrites with astonishment. These were the very people who had sworn solemn oaths by Allah that they were with the believers, that they shared their faith and their cause. Now their double loyalty has been exposed. The question carries amazement: after all those oaths, was this where they stood all along?\n\nThe verse then gives the verdict: “Their deeds have been in vain, so they have become losers.” The word “deeds” is important. The hypocrites were not lazy — they prayed, they attended gatherings, they made oaths, they took part in outward religious life. But because their hearts were not with Allah, all of it went to waste. The Quran says elsewhere that the deeds of those who disbelieve are like a mirage or scattered dust — they look like something, but they carry no weight before Allah. An action built on hypocrisy is a building without a foundation.\n\nAnd so they “have become losers.” They lost this world, because they gained nothing from their double game; and they lost the Hereafter, because their deeds were hollow. The believers, by contrast, are told to be truthful in what they say and consistent in what they do, so that their deeds will not be among those that are wasted. An oath is a serious thing, and the Quran is showing how serious it is by recording these oaths and their outcome.",
  "54": "“O believers! Whoever among you abandons their faith, Allah will replace them with others who love Him and are loved by Him.” This is one of the most powerful verses in the chapter. It tells the believers that the religion does not depend on any particular people. If some abandon their faith, Allah is not left without worshippers. He will bring others — and the verse describes them with a love that flows in both directions: they love Allah, and Allah loves them. This mutual love is the heart of faith. Commentators mention that the verse was connected in the minds of the early Muslims with the wars of apostasy after the death of the Prophet, when many tribes turned back and Allah replaced them with those who stood firm.\n\nThe verse then describes these people: “They will be humble with the believers but firm towards the disbelievers, struggling in the Way of Allah; fearing no blame from anyone.” Four qualities are named. First, humility with the believers — no arrogance among themselves. Second, firmness toward the disbelievers — no weakness in the face of the enemies of the faith. Third, struggling in the Way of Allah — striving with life, wealth, and effort. Fourth, fearing no blame from anyone — not bending because people criticize them. Another chapter makes the same point about replacement: “If you ˹still˺ turn away, He will replace you with another people. And they will not be like you.”\n\nThen the verse concludes: “This is the favour of Allah. He grants it to whoever He wills. And Allah is All-Bountiful, All-Knowing.” Being among those who love Allah and are loved by Him is not something a person earns by birth or tribe. It is a favour Allah places in hearts He knows are sincere. The verse both warns and encourages: warn those who might abandon the faith, and encourage those who hold firm that their standing is a gift from the All-Knowing.",
  "55": "“Your only guardians are Allah, His Messenger, and fellow believers— who establish prayer and pay alms-tax with humility.” After the previous verses warned against taking Jews and Christians as guardians, this verse answers the question: then who are your true guardians? The answer names three: Allah, His Messenger, and the fellow believers. The word “only” is decisive — there is no fourth option. The believer’s protection and loyalty belong to Allah first, then to His Messenger, then to the community of believers.\n\nThe verse then describes those believers with two qualities: they “establish prayer and pay alms-tax with humility.” Prayer and alms-tax appear together throughout the Quran as the two pillars of worship and charity — the bond with Allah and the bond with people. And the word “humility” adds the inner state: they do these deeds while bowing their hearts before Allah, not proudly, not to be seen. Some commentators mention a report connecting this verse to Ali ibn Abi Talib, who was said to have given charity while bowing in prayer. Scholars note that the report is mentioned in the books of tafsir, but the wording of the verse is general, and the meaning stands on its own: the true guardians of the believers are those who worship Allah with humility and give charity with sincerity.\n\nThe verse teaches an important lesson about where a Muslim places his trust. A believer may live among people of other faiths, deal with them fairly, and treat them kindly — but his guardianship, his deepest loyalty, and his protection belong to Allah, His Messenger, and his fellow believers. When loyalty is placed in the right hands, the believer is never truly alone.",
  "56": "“Whoever allies themselves with Allah, His Messenger, and fellow believers, then it is certainly Allah’s party that will prevail.” The previous verse named the true guardians: Allah, His Messenger, and the believers. This verse now states the result of taking them as guardians. The words “whoever allies themselves with” describe a deliberate choice — the person who places his loyalty with Allah, the Messenger, and the believers. And the result is stated with complete certainty: that person belongs to “Allah’s party,” and Allah’s party will prevail.\n\nThe word “prevail” covers both meanings: victory in this world and success in the Hereafter. The believer who stands with Allah may face hardship, but he is on the winning side of history, because the final outcome belongs to Allah. The same phrase appears at the end of another chapter, which describes the believers and then says: “They are the party of Allah. Indeed, Allah’s party is bound to succeed.” There, the verse also describes what loyalty to Allah looks like: people who do not love those who defy Allah and His Messenger, even if they are their parents or children — because faith has been instilled in their hearts.\n\nThis verse comes right after the warning against taking the Jews and Christians as guardians. So it completes the picture: do not take them as guardians, and instead take Allah, His Messenger, and the believers — because the first path leads to loss, and the second leads to victory. The choice is placed before every believer, in every generation. Whose party do you belong to? The answer is shown by where a person places his deepest loyalty when loyalties conflict.\n\nThe verse is also a comfort: the believers of that time were a small community surrounded by powerful empires and hostile tribes. Yet the Quran promised them victory — not because of their numbers, but because of whose party they belonged to.",
  "57": "“O believers! Do not seek the guardianship of those given the Scripture before you and the disbelievers who have made your faith a mockery and amusement.” The verse extends the warning even further. The previous verses spoke about the Jews and Christians generally. This verse speaks about those among them — and among the disbelievers — who mock the faith. The warning is not to take them as guardians at all, and the reason is stated: they have “made your faith a mockery and amusement.” They laugh at the religion, make jokes of its rituals, and treat its followers as fools. To take such people as protectors is to place your hand in the hand of someone who is laughing at you.\n\nMocking the messengers is not new. Another chapter tells the Prophet: “˹Other˺ messengers had already been ridiculed before you ˹O Prophet˺, but those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule.” The mockers of the past were destroyed by the very thing they laughed at. The Quran repeats this pattern to comfort the believers: mockery is a sign of ignorance, not of strength.\n\nThen comes the condition: “And be mindful of Allah if you are truly believers.” Mindfulness of Allah is the guard against drifting into the company of mockers. A person who remembers that Allah sees him will not seek protection from those who insult his religion. The verse connects guardianship with mindfulness: the heart that is truly aware of Allah chooses its loyalties carefully.\n\nThe verse is not a command to hate people of other faiths or to treat them badly. It is a command about guardianship — about whom a believer trusts with his religious loyalty and his security. Kindness in daily dealings is a different matter, as other verses make clear. But loyalty, protection, and the deepest trust belong only to those who do not mock the faith.",
  "58": "“When you call to prayer, they mock it in amusement.” The verse now gives a concrete example of the mockery mentioned in the previous verse. The call to prayer — the adhan — is the public announcement of Islam: the declaration that Allah is greatest, that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad is His Messenger. Five times a day, the call rings out. And the verse says: when the believers make this call, some people mock it and treat it as a joke. This is the mockery of the faith in action — not a private insult, but public amusement at the most public act of worship.\n\nWhy would anyone mock the call to prayer? The verse answers: “This is because they are a people without understanding.” The mockery does not come from knowledge; it comes from ignorance. A person who understood what the adhan means — that it calls people to the worship of the One God, to success, to prayer — would not find it funny. Only a heart empty of understanding turns the greatest announcement into a joke. The same is true of every form of religious mockery: it is the laughter of those who do not understand what they are laughing at.\n\nThe verse carries a quiet comfort for the believers. The mockers are described as people “without understanding” — their opinion carries no weight. The believers are not to be shaken by their amusement, nor to stop the call. The adhan continued, and the mockery continued — and the religion continued to grow, until the call to prayer was heard in lands the mockers never imagined.\n\nThere is also a lesson in how the Quran deals with mockery: it does not answer mockery with mockery. It explains, warns, and moves on. The believer who is mocked for his religion should hold firm, because the mockery is the problem of the mocker, not of the faith.",
  "59": "“Say, O Prophet, “O People of the Book! Do you resent us only because we believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us and what was revealed before—while most of you are rebellious?”” The Prophet is told to put a question to the People of the Book — a question that exposes the emptiness of their hostility. The question is: what exactly is our crime? The believers are accused, mocked, and opposed. But their offence, the verse says, is nothing more than this: they “believe in Allah and what has been revealed to us and what was revealed before.” They believe in Allah, in the Quran revealed to Muhammad, and in the earlier scriptures revealed before him. That is the entire crime.\n\nThe question is devastating because it shows the contradiction in the accusers. The People of the Book claimed to believe in Allah and in revelation. Yet they resented a community that believed in Allah and in all revelation — including their own. Their resentment was not based on the believers’ unbelief; it was based on their belief. The verse ends with the sting: “while most of you are rebellious.” The majority of them were not faithful followers of their own Books; they were rebellious against the very revelation they claimed to honour.\n\nThis is the pattern of those who oppose the truth: they cannot name a real crime, so they attack the faith itself. The Quran teaches the believers to answer such hostility with clarity — to state what they believe without shame, and to let the question stand.\n\nThe verse also teaches the Muslim position toward the earlier scriptures: they are believed in, honoured, and confirmed. The Quran does not tell Muslims to deny the Torah and the Gospel; it tells them to affirm what Allah revealed before, and to recognize that the Quran is their confirmation and guardian, as the coming verses will explain.",
  "60": "“Say, O Prophet, “Shall I inform you of those who deserve a worse punishment from Allah than the rebellious? It is those who earned Allah’s condemnation and displeasure—some being reduced to apes and pigs and worshippers of false gods. These are far worse in rank and farther astray from the Right Way.”” The whole verse is one speech commanded to the Prophet — a question and its answer together. The question is a challenge: you are angry at the believers and call them worthy of punishment. Let me tell you who truly deserves a worse punishment than the people you side with. The answer then names them: “It is those who earned Allah’s condemnation and displeasure—some being reduced to apes and pigs and worshippers of false gods.”\n\nThis points back to the history of the Children of Israel. Among them were people who broke the Sabbath and were punished by being transformed — as the Quran says in an earlier chapter: “We said to them, “Be disgraced apes!”” The same transformation is mentioned again in another chapter: “But when they stubbornly persisted in violation, We said to them, “Be disgraced apes!”” Some commentators also mention that “pigs” refers to those who earned Allah’s displeasure, and “worshippers of false gods” refers to those who worshipped idols — like the calf. The exact details of which group became what are matters the commentators discuss, but the point of the verse is clear: these people earned Allah’s condemnation and His displeasure in this world, and their punishment was severe.\n\nThe verse concludes: “These are far worse in rank and farther astray from the Right Way.” The people the People of the Book were siding with — or the rebellious among them — are not the ones favoured by Allah. Those who earned His wrath are worse in standing and more lost than anyone the believers could be accused of being.\n\nThe verse teaches the believers not to be shaken by the opinions of those who condemn them. The real measure of a person is not what people say about him, but how Allah sees him. And it teaches that privilege — like being the descendants of prophets — is no protection when a people earn Allah’s displeasure.",
  "61": "“When they come to you ˹believers˺ they say, “We believe.” But they are committed to disbelief when they enter and when they leave.” The verse describes the hypocrites among the People of the Book. When they come to the believers, they say the words “We believe” — the same words the believers say. But their faith is only a greeting. The verse says they are “committed to disbelief when they enter and when they leave” — their hearts were fixed on disbelief as they came in and as they went out. The words of belief entered with them, but the belief itself never entered their hearts.\n\nThe verse exposes the difference between words and faith. Saying “I believe” is easy; believing is something else. The hypocrite uses the language of faith as a cover. He says “We believe” because it opens doors, calms suspicion, and gives him a place among the believers — while his real loyalty remains elsewhere. This is why the Quran so often tests the claim of faith with action and with the heart, not with the tongue.\n\nThen the verse adds: “And Allah knows what they hide.” This is both a threat and a comfort. It is a threat to the hypocrites: your hidden hearts are not hidden from Allah. And it is a comfort to the sincere believers: you do not need to expose every hypocrite or prove every suspicion, because Allah knows, and His knowledge is the final accounting. The believers are not commanded to read hearts — that is Allah’s right alone. They are commanded to deal justly with what is apparent, and to leave what is hidden to the One who knows it all.\n\nThe verse connects with the earlier description in this chapter of those who “say, “We believe” with their tongues, but their hearts are in disbelief.” The Quran keeps returning to this double-faced behaviour because it is the most dangerous enemy of the community from within.",
  "62": "“You see many of them racing towards sin, transgression, and consumption of forbidden gain.” The verse turns from the hypocrites to another group — many of the People of the Book. The word “racing” is the same word used earlier in the chapter for those who race to disbelief and for the hypocrites who race to take the disbelievers as guardians. Here they race toward three things: sin, transgression, and forbidden gain. Sin is the violation of Allah’s limits. Transgression is going beyond the bounds in dealing with people — oppression and injustice. Forbidden gain is wealth taken by unlawful means — bribery, usury, cheating, and the like. The verse gathers the three: wrong against Allah, wrong against people, and wrong in the pursuit of wealth.\n\nThe verse then says: “Evil indeed are their actions!” The exclamation is a verdict. Their racing is not a harmless habit; it is an evil their own deeds testify against. The previous verses had already mentioned that they “consume forbidden gain” — the same phrase — and this verse shows that this consumption is not occasional but a race: they hurry toward it as if competing.\n\nThe verse teaches that evil is not only doing what is forbidden; it is also the attitude of hurrying toward it. A person who hesitates before sin, who feels its weight, is very different from one who races toward it eagerly. And the Quran warns the believers not to be deceived by the apparent success of those who gather forbidden wealth. Their actions are evil, whatever their outward state — and evil actions carry their own end.\n\nThe next verse will add another painful layer: their religious teachers see all this and say nothing.",
  "63": "“Why do their rabbis and scholars not forbid them from saying what is sinful and consuming what is unlawful?” The question is a rebuke — aimed not at the sinners, but at their teachers. The rabbis and scholars were the religious leaders of the People of the Book. They knew the Torah, they taught the people, and they carried the responsibility of guiding them. Yet, the verse asks, why do they not forbid the sinful speech and the unlawful consumption that the previous verse described? The question carries its own answer: they should have forbidden it, and their silence was a failure.\n\nThe verse then delivers its verdict: “Evil indeed is their inaction!” Notice the word “inaction.” The previous verse called the sinners’ deeds evil; this verse calls the scholars’ silence evil. A scholar who watches his people sin and says nothing is not neutral — his silence is itself a sin. The Prophet taught the same duty to his own community in a hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim: whoever among you sees an evil, let him change it with his hand; if he cannot, then with his tongue; if he cannot, then with his heart — and that is the weakest level of faith. The duty of forbidding wrong is placed on the scholars first, because they are the ones who know.\n\nThe verse also explains why communities fall: corruption spreads when those who know remain silent. When teachers fear people more than they fear Allah, when they protect their comfort instead of their duty, the people are left to race toward sin without a voice of warning. The Quran exposed this failure among the rabbis and scholars of the past so that the scholars of this community would not repeat it. Knowledge carries a duty, and silence in the face of evil is not wisdom — it is participation by neglect.",
  "64": "“˹Some among˺ the Jews said, “Allah is tight-fisted.”” The verse records a shocking statement. Some Jews said that Allah is stingy — that He withholds His bounty. The Quran answers with a curse of justice: “May their fists be tied and they be condemned for what they said.” The punishment matches the claim: they described Allah as tight-fisted, so their own hands are pictured as tied. This is the Quran’s way of turning their own words back on them.\n\nThen comes the correction: “Rather, He is open-handed, giving freely as He pleases.” Allah’s giving is not limited by anything. He gives without measure, to whomever He wills, whenever He wills. Another verse makes the same point about human nature: “Even if you were to possess the ˹infinite˺ treasuries of my Lord’s mercy, then you would certainly withhold ˹them˺, fearing they would run out—for humankind is ever stingy!” It is humans who hold back out of fear of loss; Allah’s treasures never run out.\n\nThe verse then says something striking: “That which has been revealed to you ˹O Prophet˺ from your Lord will only cause many of them to increase in wickedness and disbelief.” The Quran was not sent to increase wickedness — it is guidance and mercy. But when a people reject it, the very revelation becomes a proof against them, and their rejection pushes them deeper. The same sun that melts wax hardens clay.\n\nThen the verse describes their state: “We have stirred among them hostility and hatred until the Day of Judgment.” Like the earlier verse about the Christians, their own divisions are part of their punishment. “Whenever they kindle the fire of war, Allah puts it out.” Whenever they tried to ignite war against the Muslims, Allah extinguished their plans. “And they strive to spread corruption in the land. And Allah does not like corruptors.” Corruption is their striving, and Allah’s dislike is their end.",
  "65": "“Had the People of the Book only been faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺, We would have certainly absolved them of their sins and admitted them into the Gardens of Bliss.” The verse is a door of mercy standing right after harsh words. The previous verses described the claims, the mockery, and the corruption among the People of the Book. Now the Quran says: had they been faithful and mindful of Allah — had they truly believed and feared Him — then the result would have been forgiveness and Paradise. Their problem was not that Allah had closed the door on them. Their problem was their own refusal to walk through it.\n\nThe verse names two conditions: “faithful” and “mindful ˹of Allah˺.” Faithfulness is belief that holds; mindfulness is awareness that guides action. Where these two exist, Allah promises two results: absolution of sins — the erasing of the past — and admission into the Gardens of Bliss — the reward of the future. Forgiveness and Paradise: the pair the Quran repeats for those who believe and do good.\n\nThis verse shows that the Quran’s criticism of the People of the Book was never a criticism of their existence as a people. It was a criticism of unfaithfulness and lack of mindfulness. The same conditions are offered to them as to anyone. The door was open, and the next verse will describe what they would have received had they walked through it — not only in the Hereafter, but in this world as well.\n\nThere is a lesson for every reader: the promises of Allah are tied to conditions, and the conditions are within human reach. No one is excluded from mercy by his past, only by his refusal to be faithful and mindful now.",
  "66": "“And had they observed the Torah, the Gospel, and what has been revealed to them from their Lord, they would have been overwhelmed with provisions from above and below.” The verse continues the conditional promise of the previous verse. The conditions were not only faith in the heart; they were also observance in practice — following the Torah, the Gospel, and the final revelation sent to them. The phrase “what has been revealed to them from their Lord” points to the Quran, because the earlier Books themselves promised the coming of a final Messenger, and the People of the Book were commanded to believe in him when he came.\n\nThe reward is stated in worldly terms: “they would have been overwhelmed with provisions from above and below.” Provisions from above — rain and the blessings of the sky; provisions from below — the growth of the earth. Their land would have been fertile and their life abundant. The same promise appears in another chapter: “Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺, We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth.” Faithfulness and mindfulness open the gates of provision. The Quran teaches that the connection between obedience and provision is real — not that every believer becomes rich, but that blessing and abundance follow the obedience of communities.\n\nThen the verse ends with fairness: “Some among them are upright, yet many do nothing but evil.” The Quran does not paint with one brush. Among the People of the Book there were upright people — those who had believed and followed the truth. But they were a few; the many were doing evil. This balanced statement matters: it prevents the reader from judging a whole community, while still facing the truth about the majority.\n\nAnd the mention of the “upright” prepares the coming verse, which will speak of the believers among them — those who truly believed in Allah and the Last Day and did good.",
  "67": "“O Messenger! Convey everything revealed to you from your Lord.” The verse is a command to deliver the entire message — not part of it, not the comfortable parts, but everything. The next sentence shows how serious this is: “If you do not, then you have not delivered His message.” Delivering half the message is delivering none of it. A messenger cannot pick and choose: he must convey what Allah revealed, whether people accept it or not. Commentators explain that this verse is the strongest statement of the duty of conveying the revelation completely, without fear of any person.\n\nThen comes the protection: “Allah will certainly protect you from the people.” Reports record that before this verse, the Prophet was guarded by his companions; after it was revealed, he told them that Allah Himself had taken over his protection, and the guards were no longer needed. The protection of Allah is the only protection a messenger needs. The verse was revealed at a time when the Prophet faced the hostility of the Quraysh and the scheming of the People of the Book, and it placed his security in Allah’s hands alone. In the Farewell Pilgrimage, at the end of his life, the Prophet asked the gathered thousands: “Have I not conveyed the message?” and they answered that he had — a scene recorded in Sahih Muslim.\n\nThe verse ends: “Indeed, Allah does not guide the people who disbelieve.” The disbelievers will not be guided while they persist in their rejection — but this is not a reason for the messenger to stop conveying; it is a reason to keep conveying until the truth is clear.\n\nThe verse teaches every bearer of the truth the same lesson: convey what you know of the truth fully, fear Allah and not people, and trust Allah for your protection. The duty is to deliver; the guidance is Allah’s.",
  "68": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “O People of the Book! You have nothing to stand on unless you observe the Torah, the Gospel, and what has been revealed to you from your Lord.”” The verse tells the People of the Book the truth about their own position: they have no firm ground — nothing to stand on — unless they actually observe their own Books and the final revelation. The phrase “nothing to stand on” is strong: their claim to be the people of the Book is empty if they do not follow the Book. Claiming scripture without observing it is standing on air.\n\nThe three things they must observe are named: the Torah, the Gospel, and “what has been revealed to you from your Lord” — the Quran, the revelation that came to Muhammad. This is the same threefold command given two verses earlier. The Torah and the Gospel themselves carried the news of the final Messenger and commanded belief in him; so observing them fully includes accepting what they pointed to.\n\nThen the verse repeats a striking phrase: “And your Lord’s revelation to you ˹O Prophet˺ will only cause many of them to increase in wickedness and disbelief.” The same words appeared in the verse about those who said, “Allah is tight-fisted.” The Quran, sent as mercy, becomes a source of increase in evil for those who reject it — because every rejected verse adds to their proof against them. Their wickedness grows as their opposition grows.\n\nThe verse ends with comfort for the Prophet: “So do not grieve for the people who disbelieve.” The Prophet is told not to carry the grief of their rejection. He delivered the message; their refusal is their own choice and their own loss. A caller to the truth must learn this: you are responsible for conveying, not for converting.",
  "69": "“Indeed, the believers, Jews, Sabians and Christians—whoever ˹truly˺ believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.” The verse lists four groups: the believers, the Jews, the Sabians, and the Christians. The Sabians were a religious community mentioned in the Quran; scholars differ in their descriptions of them — some say they were a people who worshipped the angels or the stars, others describe them differently — but the Quran names them as one of the communities addressed. The verse then lays down the condition that applies to all of them: “whoever ˹truly˺ believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good.”\n\nThe same statement appears in an earlier chapter with almost the same words: “Indeed, the believers, Jews, Christians, and Sabians—whoever ˹truly˺ believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good will have their reward with their Lord. And there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.” The repetition shows that the Quran is not mocking any community by name; it is laying down a universal standard: true belief in Allah, belief in the Last Day, and good deeds.\n\nHow is this verse understood? The scholars explain that it speaks about the sincere followers of their own prophets before the coming of Muhammad — the faithful Jews and Christians who believed in Allah and the Last Day and did good in the time of their own messengers, and the upright ones mentioned in the previous verse. After the coming of the final Messenger, faith requires believing in him, because he is the messenger of this era, and the previous scriptures themselves commanded belief in him. Some reports mention that Salman al-Farisi asked the Prophet about his Christian friends, and a similar verse was revealed in answer. The promise is real for whoever truly met its conditions in his own time.\n\nThe verse teaches two things: Allah judges by truth and sincerity, not by names; and the final criterion of acceptance is genuine faith and good deeds, whatever label a person carried.",
  "70": "“Indeed, We took a covenant from the Children of Israel and sent them messengers.” The verse turns back to the history of the Children of Israel to explain why they were addressed so strongly throughout this chapter. They had received a covenant from Allah — a binding agreement — and messengers were sent to them one after another. The pattern of their history is described in another chapter: “Indeed, We gave Moses the Book and sent after him successive messengers. And We gave Jesus, son of Mary, clear proofs and supported him with the holy spirit. Why is it that every time a messenger comes to you ˹Israelites˺ with something you do not like, you become arrogant, rejecting some and killing others?”\n\nThe next sentence of this verse summarizes that tragic history: “Whenever a messenger came to them with what they did not desire, they denied some and killed others.” The messengers brought what Allah commanded — and much of it did not match the desires of the people. So they treated the messengers according to their own taste: the ones whose message they disliked they denied; some they even killed. To deny a messenger is to reject the message; to kill a messenger is to wage war on the One who sent him. And the Quran records that among those killed were prophets sent to their own people.\n\nThe verse is a warning to every community that receives messengers: the messenger comes with the truth, not with your desires. The Children of Israel were not destroyed because the messengers were wrong; they were destroyed because they measured the messengers by their own wishes. The lesson for the believers of this community is to receive the truth with humility — to weigh their desires against revelation, not revelation against their desires. The covenant is a test, and the test is whether a people will follow what Allah sent, even when it crosses their wishes.",
  "71": "“They thought there would be no consequences, so they turned a blind eye and a deaf ear.” The verse continues the history of the Children of Israel and exposes the root of their crimes: they thought there would be no consequences. They imagined that their denial and killing of messengers would pass without punishment — that they could sin and nothing would happen. Because of that false sense of security, they “turned a blind eye and a deaf ear”: they chose not to see the signs and not to hear the truth. Blindness and deafness here are not physical; they are the blindness and deafness of the heart that refuses to look and refuses to listen.\n\nThen comes a surprising turn: “Yet Allah turned to them in forgiveness ˹after their repentance˺, but again many became blind and deaf.” Despite their crimes — denying some messengers and killing others — Allah did not destroy them all. He turned to them in forgiveness when they repented. His mercy is immense. But the story repeats: again many of them became blind and deaf. They returned to the same blindness after being forgiven. This is the deepest tragedy the verse records: not the first fall, but the return to the same fall after mercy.\n\nThe verse ends: “And Allah is All-Seeing of what they do.” Their blind eyes are seen by the All-Seeing; their deaf ears are heard by the All-Hearing. Nothing they did escaped His sight, and nothing of what they do now is hidden.\n\nThe verse teaches a lesson about the danger of feeling safe from consequences. When a person sins and believes no one sees and nothing will happen, he walks into deeper darkness. And it teaches the greatness of Allah’s mercy — forgiveness after repentance is real — while warning that mercy is not an excuse to repeat the sin. The one who sins, repents, and returns to the same sin is like those described here: blind and deaf again.",
  "72": "“Those who say, “Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,” have certainly fallen into disbelief.” The verse repeats a verdict that appeared earlier in this chapter, where the Quran said: “Indeed, those who say, “Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,” have fallen into disbelief.” The repetition is not accidental. The claim that Jesus is Allah is so serious that the Quran states its verdict twice in one chapter — once when debating the claim, and once when showing what true faith requires. The claim makes the Messiah into the Creator, and that is a lie against both Allah and Jesus.\n\nThe verse then quotes the Messiah’s own words: “The Messiah ˹himself˺ said, “O Children of Israel! Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord.”” Jesus himself told his people to worship Allah alone — and he called Allah “my Lord and your Lord,” placing himself among the worshippers, not beside the worshipped. The same words of Jesus appear in other chapters of the Quran: “Surely Allah is my Lord and your Lord. So worship Him ˹alone˺. This is the Straight Path.” A person who claimed to be Allah would never say such a thing. Jesus said it, so the claim of his followers contradicts their own prophet.\n\nThen the verse states the great barrier: “Whoever associates others with Allah ˹in worship˺ will surely be forbidden Paradise by Allah. Their home will be the Fire.” Associating partners with Allah — shirk — is the one sin that blocks Paradise. Another verse says: “Indeed, Allah does not forgive associating others with Him ˹in worship˺, but forgives anything else of whoever He wills.” The verse does not say that every Christian is automatically in the Fire forever; it says that whoever dies upon associating others with Allah will be forbidden Paradise. The one who truly worships Allah alone is promised Paradise; the one who worships other than Him closes that door on himself.\n\nThe verse ends: “And the wrongdoers will have no helpers.” On the Day of Judgment, those who wronged themselves by worshipping other than Allah will look for helpers — and find none. Neither Jesus, nor Mary, nor any idol, nor any intercessor they trusted can help them then.",
  "73": "“Those who say, “Allah is one in a Trinity,” have certainly fallen into disbelief.” The previous verse dealt with the claim that Jesus is Allah. This verse deals with the doctrine of the Trinity — the belief that Allah is three in one. The Quran rejects this with complete clarity: “There is only One God.” The Quran does not enter into the details of how the Trinity is defined by different Christian groups; it states the fundamental truth: God is one, not three. The same command appears in an earlier chapter: “And do not say, ‘Trinity.’ Stop!—for your own good. Allah is only One God.” The word “Stop!” is striking — the Quran calls the believers to stop at the edge of this claim before going further into error.\n\nThe verse then warns: “If they do not stop saying this, those who disbelieve among them will be afflicted with a painful punishment.” The warning is attached to persistence: “if they do not stop.” This shows that the door of turning back is open — the next verse will make that door explicit. But those who keep insisting on the Trinity, rejecting the clear truth of Allah’s oneness, face a painful punishment.\n\nThe verse is careful in its wording: “those who disbelieve among them.” Not every Christian is being judged by this verse; it addresses those who persist in the claim after the truth has been made clear to them. The Quran’s aim is not to condemn people but to call them to the truth — the truth that Allah is One, that He has no partner, no son, and no equal.\n\nFor the believer, the verse is a reminder of the heart of the faith: the oneness of Allah. All the details of worship, law, and life rest on this single foundation. Whoever builds on the oneness of Allah has built on solid ground; whoever lets any being share in Allah’s lordship has undermined everything.",
  "74": "“Will they not turn to Allah in repentance and seek His forgiveness?” The question is an invitation wrapped in a rebuke. The previous two verses described the terrible claims — that Allah is the Messiah, that Allah is one in a Trinity — and warned of disbelief and punishment. But the Quran does not close the door after the warning. It immediately asks: will they not turn back? The question is asked with hope, as if saying: after all this, will they not repent? The door is right there.\n\nThe words “turn to Allah in repentance” and “seek His forgiveness” describe the two movements of returning: turning away from the false claim, and asking Allah to cover the past. The verse does not say the claim is acceptable. It says the claim can be left. A person who has said something false about Allah can renounce it, turn to Allah, and ask for forgiveness. That is the meaning of repentance: leaving the sin and returning to the Lord.\n\nThen comes the promise: “And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The two names are the answer to the question. Why should they repent? Because the One they have wronged with their words is the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. He does not refuse those who return. The same pair of names closed the verses about the repentant bandit and the repentant thief in this very chapter. Over and over, the Quran places the worst sins next to the widest mercy.\n\nThe verse teaches every reader the same lesson: no statement, no belief, and no past is beyond Allah’s forgiveness if a person truly turns back. Despair of Allah’s mercy is itself a sin. The question “will they not turn?” is asked to every heart that reads it. The answer is in the reader’s hands.",
  "75": "“The Messiah, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger.” The verse now takes the argument to its simplest form. Jesus was a messenger — honoured, chosen, and sent by Allah — but “no more than a messenger.” The phrase “no more than” is the key: he was exactly that, and nothing beyond it. Being a messenger is a great rank, but it is a created rank. A messenger is a servant of Allah who carries a message; he is not the One who sent him.\n\nThe verse adds: “˹Many˺ messengers had ˹come and˺ gone before him.” Jesus was not unique in being a messenger; a long line of messengers came before him and passed away. He was one link in the chain, not a different kind of being. Another chapter makes the same point about the messengers: “We have certainly sent messengers before you ˹O Prophet˺ and blessed them with wives and offspring.” They were human beings who lived, married, and died.\n\nThen the verse mentions his mother: “His mother was a woman of truth.” Mary is honoured with the highest praise — a woman of truth, a siddiqah, one who believed and confirmed the truth completely. The verse praises her while at the same time making clear she was a human being, not a goddess.\n\nThen comes the decisive proof: “They both ate food.” This short sentence is the whole argument. Whatever eats and drinks is a body with needs. A body that needs food cannot be the Creator, because the Creator is free of all needs. Jesus and Mary ate — therefore they were creatures. The proof is so simple that everyone can understand it, which is the Quran’s way: the deepest truths are shown with the clearest signs.\n\nThe verse ends with amazement: “See how We make the signs clear to them, yet see how they are deluded ˹from the truth˺!” The signs are clear — the eating, the birth, the human life — yet the people turn away from the truth as if blindfolded. Clarity of the proof and turning away of the heart can exist together, and that is the tragedy the verse points to.",
  "76": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “How can you worship besides Allah those who can neither harm nor benefit you? And Allah ˹alone˺ is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.”” The whole verse is one question put into the mouth of the Prophet — a question that exposes the emptiness of false worship. The verse speaks of those who are worshipped besides Allah: the idols, the saints, the beings people call upon. And it describes them with a crushing description: they “can neither harm nor benefit you.” They cannot push away a single harm and cannot bring a single benefit. So what is the point of worshipping them?\n\nThe same argument appears in another chapter: “Do not invoke, instead of Allah, what can neither benefit nor harm you—for if you do, then you will certainly be one of the wrongdoers.” The logic is simple: worship belongs to the one who has power to give and to withhold. The false gods have no power at all — they are not even aware of their worshippers.\n\nThen comes the contrast: “And Allah ˹alone˺ is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing.” The false gods neither hear nor know. Allah hears every call and knows every heart. When the worshipper calls on Allah, the All-Hearing hears him; when he hides his need, the All-Knowing already knows it. So the believer’s worship is not wasted: it reaches the One who hears and knows.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to examine his own heart: is there anything — wealth, status, a person, a habit — that I treat as if it can harm or benefit me independently of Allah? The Quran's question exposes every hidden idol. True worship, in the heart and in action, belongs only to the One who hears and knows.",
  "77": "“Say, “O People of the Book! Do not go to extremes in your faith beyond the truth, nor follow the vain desires of those who went astray before ˹you˺. They misled many and strayed from the Right Way.”” The verse is a command to the People of the Book, and it names the disease: going to extremes in faith. Extremism here means going beyond the truth — adding to religion what Allah did not put in it. The Christians went to extremes about Jesus, raising him from messenger to god. The Jews went to extremes in the other direction, rejecting the messenger who came to them. Both are extremes, and both leave the truth in the middle.\n\nThe same warning appears in an earlier chapter: “Do not go to extremes regarding your faith; say nothing about Allah except the truth.” The Quran warns against extremism in every direction: making lawful things forbidden, adding new duties Allah never gave, or raising creatures to the rank of the Creator. The middle path is the Straight Path: to believe in the messengers as messengers, to worship Allah alone, and to follow what was revealed — neither adding nor deleting.\n\nThen the verse warns: “nor follow the vain desires of those who went astray before ˹you˺. They misled many and strayed from the Right Way.” The people of the Book are told not to follow the desires of earlier people who went astray — those who corrupted their own scriptures and led many others with them. The description “they misled many and strayed from the Right Way” shows the double damage: they went astray themselves and carried others with them.\n\nThis is why the Quran keeps telling the believers to hold firmly to revelation and to beware of following anyone’s desires — even desires dressed in religious language. The Straight Path is defined by the truth, not by the crowd, and not by the enthusiasm of the extremists.",
  "78": "“The disbelievers among the Children of Israel were condemned in the revelations of David and Jesus, son of Mary.” The verse speaks of the curse that was pronounced on the disbelievers of the Children of Israel in the earlier scriptures. David was given the Psalms, and Jesus was given the Gospel — and in both revelations, the disbelievers of Israel were condemned. The same idea appears in another chapter: “They will be stricken with disgrace wherever they go, unless they are protected by a covenant with Allah or a treaty with the people.” The condemnation was pronounced by the prophets themselves, because the prophets were sent to warn as well as to give good news.\n\nThe verse then gives the reason: “That was for their disobedience and violations.” The curse was not a random punishment. It was the result of their disobedience — refusing the commands of Allah — and their violations — crossing the limits He set. The same phrase appears in an earlier chapter about them: “This is ˹a fair reward˺ for their disobedience and violations.” Cause and effect are stated plainly: the people broke the covenant, and the consequence followed.\n\nThe verse teaches an important principle: when a community is given clear revelation and then persists in disobedience, its own prophets may be the ones who announce its condemnation. The prophets do not curse out of hatred; they speak the truth about the consequences of sin. The history of the Children of Israel is recorded in the Quran not to mock them, but to warn every community — including the Muslim community — that disobedience and violations carry consequences that no noble ancestry can cancel.\n\nThe next verse will add the detail that makes this history even more painful: they did not even forbid one another from doing evil.",
  "79": "“They did not forbid one another from doing evil.” The verse continues the description of the condemned people of Israel. Their disobedience was bad enough — but worse, they did not stop one another. The people saw evil around them and said nothing. The evil was not only in what they did; it was also in what they tolerated. A community that watches sin spread without raising a voice has already surrendered.\n\nThe verse adds the verdict: “Evil indeed was what they did!” The exclamation is aimed at the silence itself. The previous verse in this chapter asked the rabbis and scholars: why do you not forbid the sinful speech and unlawful consumption? Here the same failure is described for the whole community: they did not forbid one another. The two verses belong together — the scholars failed to lead, and the people failed to follow any voice of correction.\n\nThe Prophet taught his community to act against evil in stages, in a hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim: whoever sees an evil, let him change it with his hand; if he cannot, then with his tongue; if he cannot, then with his heart — and that is the weakest level of faith. And he warned in a hadith recorded in the collections of Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi that when people see wrongdoing and do not change it, Allah is about to punish them all. The silence of the many makes the punishment of the many.\n\nThis is why the Quran describes the failure to forbid evil as evil in itself. It is not a small omission; it is participation. A person who says nothing while his brother sins, while corruption spreads, while the weak are wronged — is part of the problem. The believer is commanded to be a voice: gentle where gentleness works, firm where firmness is needed, and at the very least, a heart that hates the evil and does not approve of it.",
  "80": "“You see many of them taking the disbelievers as allies.” The verse returns to the theme of guardianship and alliance. The previous verses in this chapter warned the believers not to take the Jews and Christians as guardians. Now we see the other side: many of the People of the Book took the disbelievers — the polytheists — as their allies. Commentators mention that the Jews of Medina made alliances with the polytheists of Arabia against the Muslims, joining whoever seemed strongest against the Prophet and his community. Their loyalty was not to truth but to power.\n\nThe verse then passes its judgment: “Truly wicked are their misdeeds, which have earned them Allah’s wrath. And they will be in everlasting torment.” Their misdeeds earned Allah’s wrath — the same wrath mentioned earlier in this chapter for those who were reduced to apes and pigs. The verse does not say that every Jew is included; it says “many of them.” But those who allied themselves with the enemies of the truth and died on that path face everlasting torment.\n\nThe verse connects with the earlier warning to the believers: “Whoever does so will be counted as one of them.” Here the Quran shows that this was not an empty threat — the People of the Book themselves demonstrated the pattern: taking disbelievers as allies was part of what earned them Allah’s wrath. The believer who reads both verses sees the same law working on both sides: alliance with the enemies of Allah carries the same consequence for everyone.\n\nThe verse also teaches that loyalty is revealed by action. A person may claim faith, but his alliances show where his heart actually stands. Who a person supports in times of conflict is one of the clearest tests of who he truly is.",
  "81": "“Had they believed in Allah, the Prophet, and what has been revealed to him, they would have never taken those ˹pagans˺ as allies.” The verse explains the root of the wrong alliance described in the previous verse. The People of the Book allied themselves with the polytheists because their belief was missing. The verse lists the three things they did not truly have: belief in Allah, belief in the Prophet, and belief in what was revealed to him. Faith is a whole: believing in Allah without believing in His Messenger, or believing in the Messenger without accepting what was revealed to him, is not the complete faith the Quran calls for.\n\nThe verse then states the direct connection: “they would have never taken those ˹pagans˺ as allies.” Real belief changes loyalties. When faith is firm in the heart, a believer cannot ally himself with those who fight the truth — because his faith tells him who his people are. The alliance with the pagans was not a separate mistake; it was the outward sign of an inward emptiness.\n\nThen comes the closing verdict: “But most of them are rebellious.” The majority chose rebellion over belief. The word “most” matters — the Quran does not say “all.” There were exceptions, the upright ones mentioned earlier in the chapter. But the general picture is clear: the many refused, and their refusal showed itself in their alliances.\n\nThe verse teaches a principle that applies to every generation: examine your alliances, and let them be examined by your faith. If a Muslim finds himself supporting, in a conflict of truth, those who oppose the believers — he must ask himself what his faith actually is. Belief is not a label worn on the outside; it is a loyalty that shows in choices.",
  "82": "“You will surely find the most bitter towards the believers to be the Jews and polytheists and the most gracious to be those who call themselves Christian.” The verse draws a surprising comparison. It tells the Prophet what he will find among the people around him: the most bitter hostility toward the believers comes from the Jews and the polytheists, while the most gracious — the closest in feeling and friendliness — are those who call themselves Christian. The verse uses the phrase “those who call themselves Christian” — describing them by their own claim, not by what the Quran affirms about them.\n\nThen the verse explains why: “That is because there are priests and monks among them and because they are not arrogant.” Two reasons are given. First, among the Christians there were priests and monks — people devoted to worship and learning — and their worshipful character made their hearts softer. Second, they were “not arrogant” — they had humility, and humility opens the heart to truth.\n\nThe historical background of these verses is well known. The Muslims who migrated to Abyssinia found protection with the Negus, the Christian ruler, who refused to hand them over to the Quraysh. Reports record that when the Quran was recited in his court, the priests and monks wept. And when the Negus died, the Prophet announced his death and prayed the funeral prayer for him — an event recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim. Later, a delegation of Christians came to the Prophet, heard the Quran, and their eyes overflowed with tears, as the coming verses will describe.\n\nThe verse is not a blanket praise of all Christians, nor a blanket condemnation of all Jews. It is a description of the reality of that time, and a lesson: harshness and arrogance close the heart to the truth, while humility and devotion open it.",
  "83": "“When they listen to what has been revealed to the Messenger, you see their eyes overflowing with tears for recognizing the truth.” The verse describes the same Christians mentioned in the previous verse — those with priests and monks among them, those without arrogance. When they heard the Quran being recited, their eyes overflowed with tears. The cause is stated clearly: “for recognizing the truth.” They wept because they recognized it — because the Quran confirmed what their own scriptures had said, and because their hearts were humble enough to accept it.\n\nThe verse connects with the famous story of the delegation from Abyssinia, reported in the books of hadith and seerah: when the Quran was recited to them, they wept and accepted it. Their tears were not the tears of defeat or fear; they were the tears of hearts meeting the truth they had been waiting for.\n\nThen their words are quoted: “They say, “Our Lord! We believe, so count us among the witnesses.”” They address Allah directly: we believe — in the Messenger and the revelation. And they ask to be counted “among the witnesses” — those who bear witness to the truth. Their faith is not a private feeling; they want it to be seen and recorded.\n\nThe verse teaches an important lesson about how the truth is received. The same Quran was heard by many: some mocked it, some fought it, some distorted it — and some wept and believed. The difference was not in the words of the Quran; it was in the state of the hearts that heard it. Humility and sincerity prepare the heart for guidance. Arrogance and mockery close it.\n\nThe next verses will continue their speech and then show their reward — a reward the Quran records so that no one doubts that sincerity is honoured, whatever a person's previous background.",
  "84": "“Why should we not believe in Allah and the truth that has come to us? And we long for our Lord to include us in the company of the righteous.”” These words continue the speech of the believing Christians that began in the previous verse. Their question is full of longing: why should we not believe? What could possibly hold us back? They see the truth, they recognize it, and they can find no reason to refuse it. The question is the opposite of the question of the rejecters. The rejecters ask: why should we believe? The sincere ask: why should we not?\n\nTheir second sentence shows what they hope for: “we long for our Lord to include us in the company of the righteous.” They do not merely want to escape punishment; they want to be raised into the company of the righteous — the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs, the good. Their hope is not for the minimum; it is for the highest company. And the word “long” shows the eagerness of their hearts.\n\nThis speech is recorded in the Quran as an example of how faith should be received: with recognition, with tears, with a question that leads to belief, and with a hope that reaches toward the righteous. The Quran quotes these people so that every later reader can hear their sincerity.\n\nThere is also a lesson in the timing. These people had been Christians — the very group whose wrong beliefs the Quran corrected in the previous verses. Yet when the truth reached them, they did not defend their old claims. They turned, believed, and asked to be counted with the righteous. The Quran never locks a person into his past; it opens the door to whoever turns to the truth. The next verse will show what Allah did with their sincerity.",
  "85": "“So Allah will reward them for what they said with Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there forever.” The verse answers the speech of the previous verses. Those Christians said: we believe, count us among the witnesses, include us with the righteous. And Allah responded with the greatest reward: Gardens under which rivers flow — the Paradise promised to the believers — where they will stay forever. The verse says He rewarded them “for what they said.” Their words were not empty: their statement of faith came from a heart that had recognized the truth, and Allah took them at their word and rewarded them beyond it.\n\nThe same promise runs through the Quran for all who believe and do good. This chapter itself opened its covenant passage with the same words: “I will certainly forgive your sins and admit you into Gardens under which rivers flow.” The reward of the sincere is one reward, promised to everyone who meets its conditions — whether he was born a Muslim, a Jew, a Christian, or anything else, as the earlier verses of this chapter explained.\n\nThen the verse adds: “And that is the reward of the good-doers.” The phrase links the reward to goodness: these people were rewarded because they did good — they believed, they testified, they sought the company of the righteous, and their faith moved their conduct. Paradise is not a prize for a claim; it is the reward of the good-doers.\n\nThe verse stands as a proof of Allah’s fairness. People who spent their lives in a false belief, but turned sincerely when the truth reached them, are not punished for their past; they are rewarded for their return. And it stands as an encouragement: it is never too late to say the words of faith with a true heart — and never too early to ask to be counted among the righteous.",
  "86": "“As for those who disbelieve and reject Our signs, they will be the residents of the Hellfire.” The verse is the mirror image of the one before it. The previous verse promised Gardens to the believers among the People of the Book. This verse names the other residence: the Hellfire — for those who disbelieve and reject Allah’s signs. The same pair appeared earlier in this chapter: “As for those who disbelieve and deny Our signs, they are the residents of the Hellfire.” The wording here is slightly different — “deny” becomes “reject,” and “they are” becomes “they will be” — but the meaning is the same. The Quran keeps placing the two pictures side by side: the Gardens and the Fire, the sincere and the rejecters.\n\nThe word “residents” is significant. The disbelievers are not described as visitors or passers-by in the Fire; they are its residents — a permanent population. The verse does not speak of those who merely made mistakes or sinned; it speaks of those who disbelieve and reject the signs — the proofs of Allah — after the signs have been shown to them.\n\nThe placement of this verse is meaningful. It comes right after the story of the good Christians who believed and were promised Gardens. The contrast teaches that what matters is not the label a person carries — Christian, Jew, believer — but the state of his heart toward the truth. The ones who recognized the truth and believed are promised Paradise. The ones who disbelieve and reject the signs are promised the Fire. The dividing line runs through belief, not through ancestry or community.\n\nEvery reader is placed in front of the same two pictures: the Gardens ahead and the Fire ahead. The direction is chosen by how he responds to the signs of Allah.",
  "87": "“O believers! Do not forbid the good things which Allah has made lawful for you, and do not transgress.” The verse turns from the People of the Book to the believers, and it warns against a different kind of extremism — the extremism of self-denial. Some of the Companions, eager for worship, had decided to go beyond what Allah commanded: reports record that some said they would not marry, some said they would not eat meat, some said they would not sleep on beds, and some even spoke of castrating themselves. When the Prophet heard of this, he gathered the people and said — as recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim — that he prayed and he slept, he fasted and he broke his fast, and he married women; and whoever turns away from his way is not of him.\n\nThe verse then states the principle: “Do not forbid the good things which Allah has made lawful for you.” What Allah has made lawful is not an obstacle to worship; it is part of the religion. The lawful food, the lawful marriage, the lawful rest — these are gifts to be received with gratitude, not enemies to be fought. Forbidding them is an addition to the religion that Allah never made.\n\nThen comes the second warning: “and do not transgress.” Extremism is transgression — going beyond the limits. Whether the transgression is in forbidding what Allah allowed or in doing what He forbade, both are the same disease: leaving the limits of Allah. The verse ends: “Indeed, Allah does not like transgressors.”\n\nThe verse teaches the balance of Islam: worship without neglecting the lawful gifts of Allah, and enjoyment of the lawful without crossing into the forbidden. The believer is not a monk and not a pleasure-seeker. He is a servant who takes from Allah’s gifts with gratitude and follows Allah’s limits with obedience. The next verse will make the positive command: eat of the good lawful things.",
  "88": "“Eat of the good, lawful things provided to you by Allah.” After the warning of the previous verse — do not forbid what Allah has made lawful — comes the positive command: eat. The verse commands the believers to enjoy the good and lawful provisions Allah has given them. The word “good” describes the food itself — pure, wholesome, beneficial — and the word “lawful” describes its status — permitted by Allah. The believer is not told to eat everything; he is told to eat what is both good and lawful. The two conditions together draw the map of food in Islam: purity and permission.\n\nThe same command appears in an earlier chapter: “O believers! Eat from the good things We have provided for you. And give thanks to Allah if you ˹truly˺ worship Him ˹alone˺.” There, eating the good is connected to giving thanks; here, it is connected to the next phrase of this verse: “And be mindful of Allah in Whom you believe.” The believer eats, and while eating — and after eating — he remains mindful of the One who provided. Gratitude and mindfulness turn a meal into worship.\n\nThis verse answers the extremists described in the previous verse. They had wanted to give up the good things of life. The Quran tells them: eat them. The lawful provisions of Allah are not a test to be avoided; they are a mercy to be received. The ascetic who refuses Allah’s gifts is not more pious than the grateful believer who enjoys them within the limits.\n\nThe verse also teaches that provision comes from Allah: the food, the health, the means — all are “provided to you by Allah.” A believer who remembers this does not become arrogant about his wealth nor despairing in his poverty. He eats with thanks, shares with others, and lives mindful of the One who provides.",
  "89": "“Allah will not call you to account for your thoughtless oaths, but He will hold you accountable for deliberate oaths.” The verse introduces the ruling on oaths. An oath is a promise sworn by Allah — “By Allah, I will do this.” The verse divides oaths into two kinds. The “thoughtless oaths” are the words that slip out in ordinary speech without real intention — the “No, by Allah” and “Yes, by Allah” of daily conversation. Aisha, the wife of the Prophet, explained this meaning: the thoughtless oath is what a person says in his speech without intending it as a binding promise. For these, the verse says, Allah will not call people to account. The “deliberate oaths” are the ones sworn with intention — a real promise made by Allah’s name. These are binding, and breaking them has a penalty.\n\nThe verse then gives the penalty: “The penalty for a broken oath is to feed ten poor people from what you normally feed your own family, or to clothe them, or to free a bondsperson.” The expiation is set on a scale that suits people of different means: feeding ten poor people with the food the person normally eats, clothing them, or freeing a slave. The phrase “from what you normally feed your own family” shows that the expiation must be real generosity — the same standard as the person's own household — not a token gesture.\n\nThen comes the easier option for those who cannot afford any of these: “But if none of this is affordable, then you must fast three days.” The verse continues: “This is the penalty for breaking your oaths.” Then the command: “So be mindful of your oaths.” The believer should not swear lightly, because oaths are serious before Allah. The Prophet taught, in a hadith recorded in Sahih Muslim: whoever swears an oath and then finds something better, let him do what is better and offer expiation for his oath. So the law both protects the seriousness of oaths and opens the door of the better action.\n\nThe verse closes: “This is how Allah makes things clear to you, so perhaps you will be grateful.” The clear rulings are themselves a mercy — the believer knows exactly what is required, and gratitude follows clarity.",
  "90": "“O believers! Intoxicants, gambling, idols, and drawing lots for decisions are all evil of Satan’s handiwork.” The verse lists four things: intoxicants — everything that intoxicates, headed by wine; gambling — games of chance played for stakes; idols — the carved stones and images worshipped besides Allah; and drawing lots for decisions — the arrows and lots used by the Arabs of ignorance to seek decisions from their idols. All four are gathered under one verdict: they are “evil of Satan’s handiwork.”\n\nThe prohibition of intoxicants came in stages, and the stages are recorded in the Quran itself. In an earlier chapter, when the believers asked about wine and gambling, the answer was: “There is great evil in both, as well as some benefit for people—but the evil outweighs the benefit.” Then another verse told the believers not to approach prayer while intoxicated. Now comes the final command: “So shun them so you may be successful.” The word “shun” is stronger than “reduce” or “avoid when possible.” It means leave them completely, keep away from them entirely. Success is tied to shunning them.\n\nReports record that when the final prohibition came, the Muslims poured out their remaining wine in the streets of Medina — and the verse of this chapter that follows, which says there is no blame on those who believed and did good before the prohibition, reassured those who had died before it while still drinking.\n\nThe verse teaches that success is not found in the pleasure of intoxication or the excitement of gambling; it is found in purity and obedience. The four things are grouped together because they belong to the same workshop: they are the handiwork of Satan, designed to destroy the servant — and the command is to shun the whole group, not to bargain with parts of it.",
  "91": "“Satan’s plan is to stir up hostility and hatred between you with intoxicants and gambling and to prevent you from remembering Allah and praying.” The verse now opens the purpose behind the prohibition. The previous verse said these things are Satan’s handiwork; this verse explains what the handiwork is for. Two goals are named. First, Satan wants to “stir up hostility and hatred between you” — through intoxicants and gambling. A person drunk loses his control and his tongue, and fights break out over nothing. Gamblers sit face to face, and one man's gain is another's loss; anger and envy burn between them. The very things people seek for pleasure and excitement become the fuel of enmity.\n\nSecond, Satan wants to “prevent you from remembering Allah and praying.” Intoxication clouds the mind so that even when a person stands to pray, he is not present. Gambling ties the heart to the game, so the hours pass without remembrance. The two harms are connected: when love of the drink and the game fills the heart, the love of Allah is pushed out. And when enmity fills a community, its unity is destroyed — and its worship suffers with it.\n\nThen comes the gentle command: “Will you not then abstain?” The question is a call, not a threat. It is as if the Quran is saying: now that you know the plan of the enemy, will you not refuse his goods? The believer who understands that intoxicants and gambling are weapons aimed at his community — at its peace and at its prayer — finds it easier to shun them. The command “will you not?” appeals to the believer’s own mind and faith.\n\nThe verse teaches a deep lesson about why Allah forbids what He forbids: the prohibitions are not arbitrary tests; they protect what is precious — the heart’s remembrance of Allah, the prayer that keeps the servant connected to his Lord, and the peace that keeps the community united.",
  "92": "“Obey Allah and obey the Messenger and beware!” The verse gathers the whole religion into three words: obey Allah, obey the Messenger, and beware. Obeying Allah means following His commands; obeying the Messenger means following the one who conveys them; and beware means keeping the heart alert against disobedience. The same command appears in another chapter with an addition: “Obey Allah and obey the Messenger and those in authority among you.” Here the command stands alone, because the verse is closing a section — the section about intoxicants and gambling — and the believers are called to obedience as the way of safety.\n\nThen comes the warning: “But if you turn away, then know that Our Messenger’s duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺ clearly.” The Prophet is not responsible for forcing anyone to obey. His duty is to deliver the message clearly, and the choice to obey or turn away belongs to the people. Another chapter says the same: “But if they turn away, then your duty ˹O Prophet˺ is only to deliver ˹the message˺ clearly.”\n\nThe verse teaches the believer two things. First, obedience to Allah and to the Messenger is one obedience — the Messenger does not command from himself; he commands what was revealed to him. Second, the responsibility of the messenger is delivery, and the responsibility of the listener is response. No one can say on the Day of Judgment: the message never reached me. It reached him clearly, and the choice was his.\n\nThe words “and beware” are the heart of the verse. Obedience without awareness becomes habit; obedience with awareness becomes worship. The believer who obeys while staying alert — fearing to slip, watching his steps — is the one the verse is addressing. The next verse will soften the tone and reassure those who were confused about the past.",
  "93": "“There is no blame on those who believe and do good for what they had consumed before ˹the prohibition˺” — the verse was revealed to answer a question that troubled the believers. When the final prohibition of intoxicants came in this chapter, some people asked: what about those who died before the prohibition while still drinking wine? The verse answers: there is no blame on them. They consumed what was lawful at the time, because the prohibition had not yet come. People are judged by the law of their time, not by a law that comes later.\n\nBut the verse immediately adds conditions: “as long as they fear Allah, have faith, and do what is good; then they believe and act virtuously, then become fully mindful ˹of Allah˺ and do righteous deeds.” The condition is repeated three times, in three levels — fearing Allah and having faith and doing good; then believing and acting virtuously; then being fully mindful and doing righteous deeds. The repetition shows that the believer must keep rising: the past is forgiven, but the present and the future must be fear of Allah, faith, and good deeds. The verse does not say “the past is forgiven, so do not worry about the present.” It says the past is forgiven for those whose present is obedience.\n\nThe verse ends: “For Allah loves the good-doers.” This is the same closing phrase that ended the earlier verse about pardoning the People of the Book: “Indeed, Allah loves the good-doers.” The love of Allah is the highest reward, and it belongs to those who combine belief with good deeds.\n\nThe verse teaches a balanced lesson about repentance and change: when Allah forbids something, those who did it before the prohibition are not blamed; but after the prohibition, the believer is expected to rise to fear, faith, mindfulness, and righteousness. The past is covered by mercy; the present is the field of action.",
  "94": "“O believers! Allah will surely test you with game within the reach of your hands and your spears to distinguish those who fear Him in secret.” The verse returns to the rules of pilgrimage. Allah says He will test the believers with game — wild animals for hunting — that comes so close it is “within the reach of your hands and your spears.” The test is: while you are in the state of pilgrimage, the game approaches you, and you must not hunt it. The test is real because the temptation is real. Commentators mention that during the campaign of Hudaybiyyah, when the Muslims were in the pilgrim state, game animals came so close that the men could have taken them easily — and they held back.\n\nThe purpose of the test is stated: “to distinguish those who fear Him in secret.” The one who obeys when no one sees him — when the game is within hand's reach and no human observer is watching — proves that his fear of Allah is real. The test of the pilgrim is the test of the unseen: obedience without witnesses.\n\nA story recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim shows the rule in practice. A companion named Abu Qatada hunted a wild donkey while some of his companions were in the pilgrim state. They did not help him hunt it, but after he caught it, they asked whether they could eat from it. The Prophet asked: did any of you point him toward it or help him? They said no. So the Prophet said: eat it. The rule was clear: the one in the pilgrim state may not hunt, and may not help another hunt — but eating what was lawfully hunted by someone not in the pilgrim state was allowed.\n\nThe verse ends with a warning: “Whoever transgresses from now on will suffer a painful punishment.” After the rule is made clear, transgression carries consequences. The next verse will give the details of the penalty.",
  "95": "“O believers! Do not kill game while on pilgrimage.” The rule is stated directly. The pilgrim — the one in the state of ihram, whether for Hajj or Umrah — is forbidden to hunt land game. The sanctity of the pilgrim state protects the animals as well as the person.\n\nThen comes the penalty: “Whoever kills game intentionally must compensate by offering its equivalence—as judged by two just men among you—to be offered at the Sacred House, or by feeding the needy, or by fasting so that they may taste the consequences of their violations.” The verse itself sets the procedure: two just men judge what animal of livestock equals the game that was killed, and the compensation is one of three things. The offender may send the equivalent animal to be offered at the Sacred House — the sanctuary of the Ka’bah — or feed the needy to the value of it, or fast in proportion. The scholars of law explain the details of how these three options are measured and when each is used; the verse leaves the measure to two just judges and to the scholars who explain it. The purpose is stated at the end: the offender will “taste the consequences of their violations” — the penalty makes the violation real.\n\nThen comes mercy: “Allah has forgiven what has been done.” What was done before the prohibition is forgiven — the same mercy shown in the verse about intoxicants. But: “But those who persist will be punished by Allah.” Persistence after the rule is clear brings punishment. And the verse closes: “And Allah is Almighty, capable of punishment.”\n\nThe verse teaches that the sacred state is honoured even in small things: the killing of a single animal while on pilgrimage is not a small matter in Allah’s sight. And it teaches the structure of Islamic law: a clear prohibition, a measured compensation, a just procedure, and a door of forgiveness for the past.",
  "96": "“It is lawful for you to hunt and eat seafood, as a provision for you and for travellers.” The verse now draws the line between the two kinds of hunting. Sea game — fish and what lives in the sea — is lawful even for the pilgrim. The Quran makes the sea a provision: for the pilgrim himself, and for travellers who pass through. The Prophet confirmed this ease when he was asked about the water of the sea and said — as recorded in the hadith collections — that its water is pure and its dead are lawful. And in a story recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim, the Prophet’s army, on a journey, was fed for days from a huge fish the sea had thrown onto the shore; when they told the Prophet, he said: eat — it is provision Allah has brought out for you.\n\nThen comes the contrast: “But hunting on land is forbidden to you while on pilgrimage.” The land game — the animals of the desert and the mountains — is the subject of the previous verses’ prohibition. The pilgrim may eat what the sea gives, but he may not hunt what the land raises. The line is drawn by Allah, and wisdom is in the drawing: the prohibition protects the pilgrim’s state of humility and the sanctity of the sacred land.\n\nThe verse ends: “Be mindful of Allah to Whom you all will be gathered.” The reminder of the gathering points beyond the rules of hunting to the end of the journey: everyone will return to Allah. The pilgrim who controls his hand from the game within reach is practicing for the greater return — the day when every person is gathered to the One who saw everything.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to receive Allah’s ease with gratitude and His limits with obedience: the sea is opened as a mercy, the land is guarded as a test, and both are from the same Lord.",
  "97": "“Allah has made the Ka’bah—the Sacred House—a sanctuary of well-being for all people, along with the sacred months, the sacrificial animals, and the ˹offerings decorated with˺ garlands.” The verse returns to the sanctities of the pilgrimage, the same list that opened this chapter. The Ka’bah is called “a sanctuary of well-being” — a place of safety and peace, a refuge for people. The sacred months — the months in which fighting was forbidden — are also sanctified. And the sacrificial animals and the garlanded offerings — the animals brought to the Sacred House — are protected on their way. The verse gathers the sacred places, the sacred times, and the sacred offerings into one picture of security.\n\nThe purpose of all this is then stated: “All this so you may know that Allah knows whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth, and that He has ˹perfect˺ knowledge of everything.” Why does the sanctity of the Ka’bah lead to knowing that Allah knows everything? Because the sanctity is a command from Allah — and only One who knows all things could set such sanctities and know that people will honour them or violate them. The one who protects the Ka’bah, the months, and the offerings is the One who sees all: nothing escapes Him in the heavens or on the earth.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer to honour the symbols of Allah — the House, the months, the rituals — not as empty customs but as commands of the All-Knowing. When a person stands before the Ka’bah, he is standing before a sign that points beyond itself: to the One who made it sacred. And it teaches that Allah’s knowledge is complete: every pilgrim, every vow, every violation is known. The sanctities are protected by Allah’s law in this world, and the knowledge of Allah is the record in the next.",
  "98": "“Know that Allah is severe in punishment and that He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The verse is short, and it holds two ends of the rope. “Know that Allah is severe in punishment” — those who violate the sanctities, who persist in transgression, who fight the truth, must know that Allah’s punishment is severe. The previous verses spoke of penalties: the compensation for killing game, the punishment of the persistent. Behind every penalty stands the One who is severe in punishment. No one should imagine that Allah’s mercy makes Him careless; His justice is real, and His punishment is not weak.\n\nThen comes the other end: “and that He is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The same Lord who is severe in punishment is the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. The two descriptions are not in conflict; they are two faces of the same perfect justice and mercy. He forgives the repentant and punishes the persistent. The verse places both before the believer so that he lives between hope and fear: hope that keeps him from despairing, and fear that keeps him from being careless.\n\nThe same pairing appears throughout the Quran. This very chapter opened with the believers being told to honour their obligations, and it has returned again and again to the pair: punishment for the transgressors, forgiveness for the repentant. The thief who repents is forgiven; the one who persists is punished. The bandit who repents before capture is spared; the one who persists faces the penalty. The verse at the end of the pilgrimage laws is the summary of the whole pattern.\n\nThe believer is commanded to “know” this — not just to hear it, but to hold it as certain knowledge that shapes his life. Knowledge of Allah’s severity keeps him from sin; knowledge of His mercy keeps him from despair. Between the two, he walks the Straight Path.",
  "99": "“The Messenger’s duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺.” The verse repeats a truth stated earlier in this chapter: the Prophet’s responsibility is delivery, not compulsion. The same words closed the verse about obeying the Messenger: “Our Messenger’s duty is only to deliver ˹the message˺ clearly.” Here the statement stands alone, and the next clause explains why the Messenger can be satisfied with delivery alone: “And Allah ˹fully˺ knows what you reveal and what you conceal.”\n\nAllah knows what people reveal — their open statements and actions — and what they conceal — their hidden intentions and secrets. The Messenger delivers the message to all alike; he cannot see the hearts. But the One who sent him sees everything. So the accounting is safe in Allah’s hands: those who accepted the message openly but hid rejection will be known; those who believed sincerely without display will be known. The Prophet does not need to expose every heart, because Allah knows them all.\n\nThe verse teaches a great comfort to every caller to the truth. The caller’s duty is to deliver the message clearly and completely — and then to leave the results to Allah. He is not responsible for the response. Some will accept, some will argue, some will pretend — and the One who knows what is revealed and what is concealed will settle everything.\n\nIt also teaches the believer honesty: since Allah knows what is hidden, hypocrisy is pointless. A person may impress people with what he reveals, but the accounting is built on what he conceals. The believer’s concern should be the hidden record — the heart that Allah sees.",
  "100": "“Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ “Good and evil are not equal, though you may be dazzled by the abundance of evil.”” The verse lays down a principle that the believer must hold firmly: good and evil are not equal. They may look alike on the surface; evil may be more numerous, more visible, more attractive, more successful in appearance. The verse admits this: “you may be dazzled by the abundance of evil.” Evil can be so plentiful that it seems normal, even victorious. But abundance does not change value. One act of good is worth more than a mountain of evil, because good comes from Allah and evil comes from the enemy of Allah.\n\nThe verse is a warning against being deceived by numbers. A believer who sees corruption everywhere — people drinking, gambling, mocking, sinning — may begin to think: everyone is doing it; it must be acceptable. The Quran says: no. The abundance of evil is a dazzle, not a proof. What is right remains right even when it is rare, and what is wrong remains wrong even when it is common.\n\nThen comes the command: “So be mindful of Allah, O people of reason, so you may be successful.” The verse calls those who think — “people of reason” — because seeing through the dazzle of evil is an act of the mind, not just the heart. Mindfulness of Allah is the protection: the one who remembers Allah weighs things by His scale, not by the crowd’s scale. And the result is success — the same success promised to those who shun the evil of Satan’s handiwork in the earlier verses of this chapter.\n\nAnother passage of the Quran tells the believer that every deed will be weighed exactly: “So whoever does an atom’s weight of good will see it. And whoever does an atom’s weight of evil will see it.” The atom’s weight is the true measure — not the noise, not the crowd, not the abundance.",
  "101": "“O believers! Do not ask about any matter which, if made clear to you, may disturb you.” The verse teaches the believers a lesson about questioning. Not every question is praiseworthy. The believers at the time of the Prophet sometimes asked about matters that Allah had left open, and the answer made things harder for them. The verse tells them: do not ask about matters that, if they were made clear and obligatory, would burden you. Some things are left open as a mercy, and asking about them can close the door of that mercy.\n\nThe Prophet demonstrated this in a famous incident recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim. A man stood up during the Farewell Pilgrimage and asked: “Is Hajj required every year?” The Prophet did not answer immediately; the man repeated the question, and the Prophet said: if I said yes, it would become obligatory, and you would not be able to do it. Then he added: leave me with what I have left you — those before you were destroyed by their excessive questioning and their disagreement with their prophets. The teaching is clear: excessive questioning of what Allah has left open brings difficulty.\n\nThen the verse gives the good kind of asking: “But if you inquire about what is being revealed in the Quran, it will be made clear to you.” Asking about what Allah is revealing — seeking to understand the revelation — is praiseworthy. The difference is between asking to know the truth and asking to test the limits.\n\nThe verse continues: “Allah has forgiven what was done ˹in the past˺. And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Forbearing.” The past questions, the past overstepping, are forgiven. Allah is forbearing — He does not rush to punish, and He accepts the return of those who stop. The believer learns: ask what is useful, accept the mercy of what is left open, and stop when the answer would only bring burden.",
  "102": "“Some people before you asked such questions then denied their answers.” The verse warns the believers by pointing to the past. Other nations before them had the same habit of excessive questioning — and when the answers came, they denied them. The questioners were not more pious for asking; they ended up rejecting the very rulings they had demanded to know. The pattern repeats itself across history: people ask, the answer comes, and the answer does not match their desires — so they deny it.\n\nThe clearest example is the story of the cow told in the second chapter. When the Children of Israel were commanded to sacrifice a cow, they asked: “Are you mocking us?” Then they asked what kind of cow it should be, and the command became more specific: “The cow should neither be old nor young but in between. So do as you are commanded!” Every extra question added a new condition, until the matter became difficult for them. Their questioning turned a simple command into a heavy one. Commentators mention this story when explaining this verse, because it shows exactly the danger the verse warns against: questions asked not for guidance but for delay and escape.\n\nThe verse is a warning to the Muslim community: do not repeat the habit of those before you. The religion came with clear commands and clear limits. Excessive questioning — especially about matters Allah has left open — does not bring ease; it brings difficulty, and it can end in denial of the very answers that come.\n\nThere is also a lesson about intention: the questioner who asks to understand is blessed; the questioner who asks to avoid or to argue harms himself. The Quran judges the same action by the heart behind it.",
  "103": "“Allah has never ordained the ˹so-called˺ baḥîrah, sâ'ibah, waṣîlah, and ḥâm camels.” The verse mentions four names from the customs of pre-Islamic Arabia. These were special categories of animals that the Arabs dedicated to their idols and then treated with strange rules. The commentators explain them: the baḥîrah was a camel whose ears were slit after she had given birth a number of times, after which she was set free and her milk and use were forbidden; the sâ'ibah was a camel set loose for the idols, allowed to graze freely without being used; the waṣîlah was an animal dedicated when it was born in a certain way; and the ḥâm was a stallion camel that was exempted from work after fathering a number of offspring. The details differ in the reports, but the meaning is the same: these were man-made rules about animals that people attributed to Allah.\n\nThe verse says plainly: “Allah has never ordained” any of this. These customs were invented — the Quran calls them fabrications: “But the disbelievers just fabricate lies about Allah, and most of them lack understanding.” The people who made these rules did not receive them from Allah; they invented them and then claimed religious authority for them. They did this out of ignorance: “most of them lack understanding.” They did not understand that religion comes from revelation, not from custom.\n\nThe verse teaches a lasting principle: no custom, however old and however respected, becomes religion simply because people practice it and attribute it to Allah. The measure of religion is what Allah actually revealed. The Arabs of ignorance had a whole system of invented rules — about animals, about women, about food — and the Quran came to sweep them away and replace them with Allah’s law. The believer must always be ready to measure inherited customs by revelation, and to leave what Allah never ordained, no matter how ancient it is.",
  "104": "“When it is said to them, “Come to Allah’s revelations and to the Messenger,” they reply, “What we found our forefathers practicing is good enough for us.”” The verse exposes the excuse of blind following. When the people of ignorance were called to Allah’s revelation and to His Messenger, their answer was not an argument — it was an appeal to their ancestors: what our forefathers practiced is good enough for us. The reply assumes that the practices of the forefathers are a proof of truth. The Quran rejects that assumption completely.\n\nThe same scene appears in an earlier chapter: “When it is said to them, ‘Follow what Allah has revealed,’ they reply, ‘No! We ˹only˺ follow what we found our forefathers practicing.’ ˹Would they still do so,˺ even if their forefathers had ˹absolutely˺ no understanding or guidance?” The question in that verse is devastating: would you follow them even if they had no understanding and no guidance? Following someone who was himself lost is not following; it is falling in a line.\n\nThen this verse adds its own question: “˹Would they still do so,˺ even if their forefathers had absolutely no knowledge or guidance?” The forefathers had no knowledge — no true knowledge of Allah — and no guidance — no message from Him. To follow them is to choose ignorance over knowledge, and error over guidance, merely because the error is older.\n\nThe verse teaches a fundamental rule of the Quran: ancestry is not authority. Truth is not true because our fathers held it, and falsehood is not false because our fathers rejected it. The believer’s loyalty is to the truth wherever it is found — and when the truth contradicts inherited custom, the truth wins. The earliest Muslims proved this: they abandoned the religion of their fathers for the revelation of Allah, and the Quran honoured them for it.",
  "105": "“O believers! You are accountable only for yourselves.” The verse is often misunderstood, so its full meaning must be seen together with the rest of the verse and with the other verses of the Quran. It says: “It will not harm you if someone chooses to deviate—as long as you are ˹rightly˺ guided.” The person who is guided is not harmed by the deviation of others — but the condition is important: “as long as you are ˹rightly˺ guided.” Being guided includes calling others to guidance. The early Muslims understood this well. When Abu Bakr recited this verse, he said — as recorded in the hadith collections — that people had placed it in the wrong place, and that he heard the Prophet say: when people see an oppressor and do not seize his hand, Allah is about to punish them all. And the Prophet told his companion Abu Tha’labah, in a hadith recorded by Abu Dawud and al-Tirmidhi, that commanding the good and forbidding the evil remains a duty — until corruption becomes general; then a person should look after himself and hold firmly to what he has of guidance.\n\nSo the verse does not cancel the duty of guiding others. It removes the burden of their choice. A believer is accountable for his own deeds: he guides, he warns, he forbids what he can — but he is not responsible for whether others accept. The one who deviates after being clearly guided carries his own deviation. The one who is guided is not harmed by it.\n\nThen comes the ending: “To Allah you will all return, and He will inform you of what you used to do.” The return to Allah is the final court. There, every person will be told what he used to do — his own deeds, not his neighbours’. The verse teaches the believer to focus his energy on his own obedience while still doing his duty of guidance — and to leave the results, and the account, to Allah.",
  "106": "“O believers! When death approaches any of you, call upon two just Muslim men to witness as you make a bequest.” The verse moves into the laws of the final moments of life. When a person is near death and wants to make a bequest — a will giving away part of his wealth — he should call two just Muslim men to witness it. The witnesses protect the rights of the heirs and the poor who are mentioned in the bequest. A bequest written before witnesses is a bequest that can be trusted.\n\nThen comes the exception: “otherwise, two non-Muslims if you are afflicted with death while on a journey.” If a person is travelling and death approaches, and no just Muslim witnesses can be found, then two non-Muslims may serve as witnesses. This is a practical allowance for a difficult situation: the testimony is taken from those who are present, even if they are not Muslims, because the alternative — no witnesses at all — is worse.\n\nThe verse then sets the safeguard: “If you doubt ˹their testimony˺, keep them after prayer and let them testify under oath ˹saying˺, “By Allah! We would never sell our testimony for any price, even in favour of a close relative, nor withhold the testimony of Allah. Otherwise, we would surely be sinful.”” If there is doubt about the witnesses, they are kept after the prayer — commentators explain this as the time after a congregational prayer, when people are gathered — and they are made to swear a solemn oath: they will not sell their testimony for any price, even for a close relative; they will not hide the testimony of Allah; and if they do, they would be sinful. The oath is designed to make false testimony heavy.\n\nThe story connected with these verses is recorded in the hadith collections: a Muslim died on a journey in a land where only non-Muslim witnesses were available, and those witnesses were later found to have taken part of his goods. The case led the Quran to set this whole procedure — the oath, the doubt, and the replacement described in the next verse.",
  "107": "“If they are found guilty ˹of false testimony˺, let the deceased’s two closest heirs affected by the bequest replace the witnesses and testify under oath ˹saying˺, “By Allah! Our testimony is truer than theirs. We have not transgressed. Otherwise, we would surely be wrongdoers.”” The verse completes the procedure of the previous verse. If the original witnesses are found to have lied — if evidence shows they took part of the bequest or concealed it — then the witnesses are replaced. The replacement witnesses are the two closest heirs affected by the bequest — the people whose rights were harmed by the false testimony. They step into the place of the liars and testify.\n\nTheir oath is also given: “By Allah! Our testimony is truer than theirs. We have not transgressed. Otherwise, we would surely be wrongdoers.” The heirs swear that their testimony is truer than the testimony of the first witnesses — that they are not attacking the dead person's bequest wrongly, that they have not crossed the limits — and they call down the guilt of wrongdoing on themselves if they lie.\n\nThis procedure is remarkable for its fairness. The first witnesses swear; if they are found guilty, the heirs swear in their place. Each side is bound by an oath in the name of Allah, and the falsehood of the first group is answered by the testimony of the injured party. The law does not leave the heirs without recourse: the lying witnesses cannot keep the property of the dead person simply because they swore first.\n\nThis is the background of the story recorded in the hadith collections about the Muslim who died on a journey and whose non-Muslim witnesses were found to have taken part of his goods — when the truth came out, the heirs swore and took back what belonged to them. The verse teaches that justice has procedures, and that even in difficult situations — a death on the road, witnesses of another faith, doubt about honesty — the law builds a path to the truth.",
  "108": "“In this way it is more likely that witnesses will give true testimony or else fear that their oaths could be refuted by those of the heirs.” The verse explains the wisdom of the whole procedure. Why did the Quran set this elaborate system of oaths and replacements? Because it “is more likely” to produce truth. The first witnesses know that if they lie, their testimony can be examined; they know that the heirs have the right to swear against them; they know that their oath can be “refuted by those of the heirs.” The liar is no longer safe: someone else can step forward and swear the truth in his place. The fear of exposure makes true testimony more likely.\n\nThe law works with human nature: people lie when lying is easy and safe. The procedure removes the safety. The oath in Allah’s name reminds the witness of Allah; the possibility of being replaced reminds him of the witnesses around him. Both the unseen and the seen are brought to bear.\n\nThen comes the command: “Be mindful of Allah and obey. For Allah does not guide the rebellious people.” The whole legal system rests on mindfulness of Allah — on hearts that fear Him. The law can arrange witnesses, oaths, and penalties, but a society that obeys Allah from within is the society the law is meant to build. And the verse warns: the rebellious — those who refuse to obey — are not guided by Allah. The guidance of Allah is for those who turn to Him.\n\nThe verse teaches the believer the spirit behind the letter of the law: the procedures exist to protect truth and rights, and the heart that fears Allah is the deepest protection of both. A witness who remembers Allah speaks the truth even without a court; a witness who forgets Allah must be guarded by the law itself.",
  "109": "“˹Consider˺ the Day Allah will gather the messengers and say, “What response did you receive?”” The verse turns from the laws of this world to the Day of Judgment. The scene is awesome: Allah gathers the messengers — the prophets who were sent to every nation — and asks them: what response did your people give you? How did they answer your call? The question is not asked because Allah does not know; it is asked to make the truth public, to open the record before everyone.\n\nAnother chapter describes the same questioning: “We will surely question those who received messengers and We will question the messengers ˹themselves˺.” Both sides are questioned: the nations who received the message, and the messengers who delivered it. Then comes the account: “Then We will give them a full account with sure knowledge—for We were never absent.” Allah was present for every conversation, every rejection, every acceptance — nothing was hidden from Him.\n\nThen the messengers reply: “They will reply, “We have no knowledge ˹compared to You˺! You ˹alone˺ are indeed the Knower of all unseen.”” Their answer is full of humility and awe. The messengers say: we have no knowledge compared to Your knowledge. You are the Knower of all unseen. They do not deny that they delivered the message; they stand in the presence of Allah's knowledge and declare that His knowledge is complete — their knowledge is nothing beside it. The answer shows the perfect humility of the prophets even at the highest rank: before Allah, all knowledge is swallowed by His knowledge.\n\nThe verse prepares the reader for the coming verses, which will show one messenger being addressed directly — Jesus, son of Mary — and the favours Allah gave him being counted one by one. The Day of Judgment is not only a day of punishment and reward; it is a day of questions, records, and the full truth being made known.",
  "110": "“And ˹on Judgment Day˺ Allah will say, “O Jesus, son of Mary! Remember My favour upon you and your mother” — the verse begins a divine speech addressed to Jesus on the Day of Judgment. The previous verse showed the messengers being questioned; now one messenger is addressed directly, and Allah begins by asking Jesus to remember the favours that were given to him and to his mother Mary. The speech is a reminder of the truth, given in front of all creation, so that no one can claim later that Jesus was more than a servant.\n\nThe first favour: “how I supported you with the holy spirit so you spoke to people in ˹your˺ infancy and adulthood.” The holy spirit is the angel Gabriel, who supported Jesus. Because of this support, Jesus spoke to people as an infant in the cradle — a miracle recorded in another chapter, where the baby Jesus declared, “I am truly a servant of Allah. He has destined me to be given the Scripture and to be a prophet.” And he spoke in adulthood as a messenger.\n\nThen the gifts: “How I taught you writing, wisdom, the Torah, and the Gospel.” All his knowledge was a gift — he was taught, so he was a student, not a teacher of Allah. Then the miracles: “How you moulded a bird from clay—by My Will—and breathed into it and it became a ˹real˺ bird—by My Will. How you healed the blind and the lepers—by My Will. How you brought the dead to life—by My Will.” Three times the phrase “by My Will” appears — the miracles happened by Allah’s will and power, not by Jesus’ own. He was the instrument; Allah was the doer. Another chapter records the same miracles with the same point: “I will make for you a bird from clay, breathe into it, and it will become a ˹real˺ bird—by Allah’s Will. I will heal the blind and the leper and raise the dead to life— by Allah’s Will.”\n\nThe verse then mentions the protection: “How I prevented the Children of Israel from harming you when you came to them with clear proofs and the disbelievers among them said, “This is nothing but pure magic.”” Despite the miracles, the disbelievers called them magic — and Allah protected Jesus from their plots. The whole verse is a complete answer to every exaggeration about Jesus: he was a servant supported by Allah, taught by Allah, empowered by Allah, and protected by Allah.",
  "111": "“And how I inspired the disciples, “Believe in Me and My messenger!”” The speech to Jesus continues, and it moves to the disciples — the close followers of Jesus. Allah says He inspired them — He placed in their hearts the call to believe in Allah and in His messenger Jesus. The disciples are honoured by this mention: they were chosen to receive the inspiration of belief and to support the messenger.\n\nTheir response is recorded: “They declared, “We believe and bear witness that we fully submit ˹to Allah˺.”” Their answer has two parts. First, “We believe” — we accept the faith. Second, “we bear witness that we fully submit ˹to Allah˺” — they testify that they are Muslims, ones who submit to Allah. The word for submission is the same root as the word Islam: the disciples of Jesus declared themselves submitters to Allah. Their religion was the same religion of submission that all the prophets brought.\n\nThe same scene appears in another chapter: “When Jesus sensed disbelief from his people, he asked, “Who will stand up with me for Allah?” The disciples replied, “We will stand up for Allah. We believe in Allah, so bear witness that we have submitted.”” And in yet another: “O believers! Stand up for Allah, as Jesus, son of Mary, asked the disciples, “Who will stand up with me for Allah?” The disciples replied, “We will stand up for Allah.”” The Quran repeats the scene because it carries a lasting lesson: true faith stands up — it does not hide. The disciples answered the call with support and testimony.\n\nThe verse also closes the speech of Allah to Jesus on the Day of Judgment: the favours are counted, the disciples are mentioned, and the truth about Jesus is sealed — a servant, a messenger, supported by Allah, followed by sincere disciples who submitted to Allah alone.",
  "112": "“˹Remember˺ when the disciples asked, “O Jesus, son of Mary! Would your Lord be willing to send down to us a table spread with food from heaven?”” This is the story that gives the whole chapter its name — Al-Ma'idah, the Table Spread with Food. The disciples were the close followers of Jesus, the ones who stood with him for Allah. The verse tells the Prophet and his community to remember this scene from the life of Jesus, and it is placed right after the Judgment Day scene of the previous verses to show the difference between the true followers of Jesus and those who later exaggerated about him.\n\nThe disciples' request was bold: they asked for a table of food to be sent down from heaven. They did not ask out of denial, as the disbelievers of other nations asked for signs. They asked as believers who wanted a sign of blessing. But notice how Jesus answered: “Jesus answered, “Fear Allah if you are ˹truly˺ believers.”” Jesus turned the request into a test. If their faith was real, they should fear Allah — and fear Allah means being careful about asking for a sign that carries great responsibility. Commentators explain that Jesus was cautious: a sign from heaven is not a toy; if people receive a clear miracle and then disbelieve, the punishment is severe — as the coming verses will show.\n\nThe exchange teaches an important lesson about asking Allah for signs. It is one thing to ask with a sincere heart for reassurance and blessing; it is another thing to demand signs out of stubbornness. The disciples were sincere, so their request was granted. But Jesus' answer reminds every believer that closeness to Allah is not measured by miracles and wonders; it is measured by fear of Allah and true faith.",
  "113": "“They said, “We ˹only˺ wish to eat from it to reassure our hearts, to verify you are indeed truthful to us, and to become its witnesses.”” The disciples answered Jesus' caution with an explanation of their intention. The word “only” shows that they were defending themselves: they did not want the table as a toy or a game; they wanted it for three reasons. First, “to eat from it” — the food itself, as a provision and a blessing. Second, “to reassure our hearts” — they wanted the certainty that comes from seeing a sign with their own eyes. This is the same longing that Abraham expressed in an earlier chapter, when he said, “My Lord! Show me how you give life to the dead,” and explained, “Yes I do, but just so my heart can be reassured.” Wanting the heart to be settled in faith is not a weakness; it is a human need, and Allah honoured it in the case of Abraham and in the case of the disciples.\n\nThird, “to verify you are indeed truthful to us” — they wanted the sign to confirm that Jesus was truly a messenger from Allah. Their hearts already believed; the sign would complete their certainty. And fourth, “to become its witnesses” — they wanted to testify to others about the miracle, so that the sign would spread beyond the small circle who saw it.\n\nTheir answer shows the difference between sincere seeking and stubborn demanding. The disbelievers demanded signs while planning to deny them; the disciples asked for a sign while intending to believe, to be reassured, and to bear witness. The same request can come from two very different hearts, and Allah treats the heart, not just the words.",
  "114": "“Jesus, son of Mary, prayed, “O Allah, our Lord! Send us from heaven a table spread with food as a feast for us—the first and last of us—and as a sign from You.”” Once the disciples explained their sincere intention, Jesus turned to Allah and prayed. Notice the beginning of his prayer: “O Allah, our Lord!” Jesus called Allah “our Lord” — his Lord and the disciples' Lord. He prayed as a servant, standing with his followers before the same God. This is the Jesus of the Quran: the servant who prays, the messenger who asks, the one who never claims lordship for himself.\n\nThe prayer asks for three things. First, the table itself: “Send us from heaven a table spread with food.” Second, the purpose: “as a feast for us—the first and last of us.” The word translated “feast” carries the meaning of a celebration, a joyful day. Commentators explain “the first and last of us” in different ways: some say it means the first generation of the disciples and the last generation of the followers of Jesus; others say it means everyone present at that time, from the first of them to the last. Whichever way it is read, the meaning is that the table was not meant for a few individuals — it was a blessing for the whole community of believers.\n\nThird, the deepest purpose: “and as a sign from You.” The food was not the real gift; the sign was. The table from heaven would be living proof that Allah accepts the prayers of His messenger. The prayer ends with the words every servant can say: “Provide for us! You are indeed the Best Provider.” Jesus asked Allah for provision, and he praised Allah as the Best Provider — because provision, in the end, comes only from Allah.",
  "115": "“Allah answered, “I am sending it down to you. But whoever among you denies afterwards will be subjected to a torment I have never inflicted on anyone of My creation.”” Allah accepted the prayer of Jesus immediately: “I am sending it down to you.” The table would come. But the answer carried a warning that shows how serious a sign from heaven is. Whoever received this miracle and then denied it would face a torment unlike any torment before it — a punishment that had never been inflicted on anyone in creation.\n\nThe reason is clear: the sign itself was unique. Food coming down from heaven before people's eyes is a direct, undeniable miracle. The prophets before had brought signs — the staff of Moses, the healing of Jesus — but a table descending from heaven with food was a sign of such clarity that denying it after seeing it would be the worst kind of stubbornness. And the Quran warns: the greater the proof, the greater the responsibility. This is the principle behind the warning: no one will be punished for what they did not know; but those who see the truth clearly and still turn away have no excuse.\n\nThe Quran does not tell us everything that happened to the table after it came down. The commentators report different accounts: some say the table came down with food, and the people were told not to be treacherous with it; some report that those who disbelieved afterwards were punished or transformed. These reports differ, and the Quran itself leaves the details untold. What the verse fixes firmly is the warning: after the sign, denial has a terrible price.",
  "116": "“And ˹on Judgment Day˺ Allah will say, “O Jesus, son of Mary! Did you ever ask the people to worship you and your mother as gods besides Allah?”” The scene moves to the Day of Judgment. Allah addresses Jesus directly, and the question is about the most serious claim ever made about him: did he ever command people to worship him and his mother as gods? Allah asks the question although He knows the answer perfectly. The purpose is public: the question is asked before all creation, so that the truth is declared openly, and every claim about Jesus is settled in front of everyone.\n\nJesus' answer is recorded in full: “He will answer, “Glory be to You! How could I ever say what I had no right to say? If I had said such a thing, you would have certainly known it. You know what is ˹hidden˺ within me, but I do not know what is within You. Indeed, You ˹alone˺ are the Knower of all unseen.”” He begins with “Glory be to You!” — declaring Allah far above everything imperfect, including the idea that a servant could be worshipped beside Him. Then he denies the charge absolutely: “How could I ever say what I had no right to say?”\n\nHis words “You know what is ˹hidden˺ within me, but I do not know what is within You” are among the most beautiful in the Quran. They draw the line between the Creator and the creature: Allah knows everything inside Jesus — every thought, every intention — while Jesus knows nothing of the unseen of Allah. The one who knows all secrets cannot be a god worshipped beside the Knower of all unseen. The verse, together with the earlier verses of this chapter, completes the Quran's answer to the worship of Jesus and Mary: he himself, on the Day of Judgment, will declare his own servanthood before all creation.",
  "117": "“I never told them anything except what You ordered me to say: “Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord!” And I was witness over them as long as I remained among them.”” Jesus continues his answer on the Day of Judgment, and now he states his whole mission in one sentence. His message was one command: “Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord!” The same words of Jesus appear earlier in this chapter: “The Messiah ˹himself˺ said, “O Children of Israel! Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord.”” His teaching was the worship of Allah alone — the same call of every prophet from Noah to Muhammad. He never asked anyone to worship him or his mother.\n\nThen Jesus describes his position among his people: “And I was witness over them as long as I remained among them.” During his life on earth, he was a witness over his followers — he saw their deeds, he taught them, he watched how they responded to the message. He could testify about what happened in his lifetime.\n\nBut then comes the boundary of his knowledge: “But when You took me, You were the Witness over them —and You are a Witness over all things.” The words “when You took me” point to the Quran's teaching that Allah raised Jesus up to Himself — he was not killed, as the Quran makes clear. After that raising, Jesus no longer witnessed what his followers did. From that moment, Allah alone was the Witness over them. And the verse adds the general truth: “You are a Witness over all things.” Jesus witnessed only during his short life; Allah witnesses everything, everywhere, at all times. The verse teaches that the prophets are honoured servants with limits, and that the only Witness whose testimony covers all things is Allah.",
  "118": "“If You punish them, they belong to You after all. But if You forgive them, You are surely the Almighty, All-Wise.”” Jesus finishes his speech on the Day of Judgment with these words — and they are the words of a true servant. He leaves the affair of his people completely in Allah's hands. He does not argue for them, and he does not condemn them. He simply states the truth: they are Allah's servants. “If You punish them, they belong to You after all.” They were created by Allah, they lived under His care, and if He punishes them, it is the justice of the Owner over His own property.\n\nThen comes the other side: “But if You forgive them, You are surely the Almighty, All-Wise.” Forgiveness is not a sign of weakness here; it is an act of the Almighty and the All-Wise. The One who forgives is the same One who is Almighty — His mercy does not reduce His power — and He is All-Wise — His forgiveness is never misplaced. This is a deep lesson about Allah's attributes: mercy and might are not opposites in Allah; both are perfect, and both work together in His wisdom.\n\nJesus says nothing about what his people deserve; he says everything about what Allah is. This is the highest form of submission: not demanding, not bargaining, but leaving the outcome entirely to Allah's justice and mercy. And for the reader, the verse carries a quiet comfort: no matter what a person has done, the final word belongs to Allah alone — the Almighty, the All-Wise — whose mercy is not forced and whose justice is never wrong.",
  "119": "“Allah will declare, “This is the Day when ˹only˺ the faithful will benefit from their faithfulness.”” The Judgment Day scene closes with the divine verdict. The word “faithfulness” here carries the meaning of truthfulness and sincerity — the same word used for the truthfulness of the prophets and the honest believers. On that Day, nothing will benefit a person except his own truthfulness: his honest faith, his sincere deeds, the loyalty of his heart to Allah. Titles will not help, ancestry will not help, claims will not help. “˹only˺ the faithful will benefit from their faithfulness” — the reward is earned by the truthfulness of the believer himself.\n\nThen comes the reward: “Theirs are Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever. Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him. That is the ultimate triumph.” The same words appear in other places of the Quran for the most honoured believers — an earlier chapter says of the foremost believers: “Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him. And He has prepared for them Gardens under which rivers flow, to stay there for ever and ever. That is the ultimate triumph.” The phrase “Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him” is the summit of the Quran's descriptions of Paradise — the Gardens are named, but the true joy is mutual pleasure: Allah's pleasure with the servant and the servant's pleasure with Allah.\n\n“That is the ultimate triumph” — the verse uses the strongest word for success. Everything before was striving; this is the triumph that ends all striving. The verse teaches the believer where to direct his effort: not to the applause of people, but to the faithfulness that Allah will count on that Day.",
  "120": "“To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything within. And He is Most Capable of everything.” The chapter of the Table Spread ends with these two sentences, and they are a perfect closing. The chapter opened with the command “O believers! Honour your obligations.” Between the opening and this ending, the Quran laid down obligations: covenants, food laws, pilgrimage rules, oaths, punishments, testimonies, and the great warning about intoxicants and gambling. And now the chapter closes by telling us who owns everything that these laws govern: “To Allah ˹alone˺ belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth and everything within.” The Owner of everything is the only One who has the right to command and forbid. The laws of the chapter are not human rules; they are the commands of the King.\n\nThe same sentence closes other passages in this chapter — it appeared in the verses about those who claim that Allah is the Messiah, and about those who claim to be Allah's beloved children. The repetition is meaningful: every time the Quran argues about a claim or a command, it returns to the same foundation — the kingdom belongs to Allah alone.\n\nThen the final words: “And He is Most Capable of everything.” The chapter that began with obligations ends with capability. Allah is capable of accepting repentance, of rewarding the faithful, of punishing the deniers, of protecting His Messenger, of bringing the table from heaven, and of gathering everyone for judgment. Nothing the chapter promised is beyond His power. The believer closes the chapter knowing that the Owner of everything is also the One who is Most Capable of everything — so his obedience is safe in the hands of the King."
};
